39533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186092511 1071 "Find where the tangent to the curve  $y=\\frac{5}{x}-2x$ at the point $(1,3)$ intersects the $y$-axis." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186092511 1071 "Find where the tangent to the curve $y = \\frac{5}{x} - 2x$ at the point $(1, 3)$ intersects the $y$-axis." 13194139533393 17592186045585 false] [17592186092511 1074 "Let $f(x)=\\frac{5}{x}-2x.$ Let's first calculate the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5}{x}-2x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{-1}-2x) \\\\  & =-5{x}^{-2}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at our point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(1)=-5(1{)}^{-2}-2=-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(3) & =-7(x-1) \\\\ y & =-7x+7+3 \\\\ y & =-7x+10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the tangent line intercepts the $x$-axis, we set $x=0$ and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-7(0)+10 \\\\ y & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(0,10).$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186092512 1068 #edb/ref 17592186089213 13194139533393 17592186045585 false] [17592186092512 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243734 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186092512 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186092512 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y = \\frac{4}{x^{3}} - x$ at the point $(1, 3)$." 13194139533393 17592186045585 false] [17592186092512 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}-x$ at the point $(1,3).$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186092512 1074 "Let $f(x)=\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}-x.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-12{x}^{-4}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(1)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1)=-12(1)^{-4}-1=-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=-13$ and the coordinates of our point $(1,3),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(3) & =-13(x-1) \\\\ y & =-13x+13+3 \\\\ y & =-13x+16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186092513 1068 #edb/ref 17592186089219 13194139533393 17592186045585 false] [17592186092513 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243615 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186092513 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186092513 1071 "Find where the normal line to the curve $f(x) = 2x^{2} + x$ at the point $(1, 3)$ intersects the $x$-axis." 13194139533393 17592186045585 false] [17592186092513 1071 "Find where the normal line to the curve $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+x$ at the point $(1,3)$ intersects the $x$-axis." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186092513 1074 "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2}+x)=4x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(1)=4(1)+1=5,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =-\\frac{1}{5}(x-1) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{5}x+\\frac{1}{5}+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{5}x+\\frac{16}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{5}x+\\frac{16}{5} & =0 \\\\ x & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at $(16,0).$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186096612 1038 #edb/ref 17592186244087 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186096612 1038 #edb/ref 17592186244089 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186096612 1038 #edb/ref 17592186244090 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186096612 1038 #edb/ref 17592186244092 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [175921