egin{aligned}{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y) & =0.1+0.25+0.25+0.05+0.15+0.2=1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Condition III is true. Because $f(x,y)$ satisfies conditions I and II, it is a valid probability mass function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186162287 1068 #edb/ref 17592186158894 13194139533393 17592186045585 false] [17592186162287 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243674 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186162287 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186162287 1071 "|  | $Y = 2$ | $Y = 4$ | $Y = 6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X = 1$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X = 2$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ |\n\nCompute $P((X, Y) ∈ {(1, 4), (1, 6), (2, 6)})$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x, y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139533393 17592186045585 false] [17592186162287 1071 "|  | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ |\n\nCompute $P((X,Y)\\in {(1,4),(1,6),(2,6)})$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186162287 1074 "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P((X,Y)\\in {(1,4),(1,6),(2,6)}) & =P((X,Y)=(1,4)) \\\\  & +P((X,Y)=(1,6)) \\\\  & +P((X,Y)=(2,6)) \\\\  & =f(1,4)+f(1,6)+f(2,6) \\\\  & =0.25+0.25+0.2 \\\\  & =0.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186162288 1068 #edb/ref 17592186158900 13194139533393 17592186045585 false] [17592186162288 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243612 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186162288 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186162288 1071 "|  | $Y = 2$ | $Y = 4$ | $Y = 6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X = 1$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X = 2$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ |\n\nCompute $P(X = 2andY \\le 4)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x, y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139533393 17592186045585 false] [17592186162288 1071 "|  | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ |\n\nCompute $P(X=2\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,Y\\le 4)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186162288 1074 "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,Y\\le 4) & =P((X,Y)\\in {(2,2),(2,4)}) \\\\  & =P((X,Y)=(2,2))+P((X,Y)=(2,4)) \\\\  & =f(2,2)+f(2,4) \\\\  & =0.05+0.15 \\\\  & =0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186162289 1068 #edb/ref 17592186158906 13194139533393 17592186045585 false] [17592186162289 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243697 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186162289 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186162289 1071 "|  | $Y = 2$ | $Y = 4$ | $Y = 6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X = 1$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X = 2$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ |\n\nCompute $P(X < 2orY = 6)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x, y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139533393 17592186045585 false] [17592186162289 1071 "|  | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ |\n\nCompute $P(X<2\\,\\,\\text{or}\\,\\,Y=6)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186162289 1074 "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<2\\,\\,\\text{or}\\,\\,Y=6) & =P((X,Y)\\in {(1,2),(1,4),(1,6),(2,6)}) \\\\  & =P((X,Y)=(1,2))+P((X,Y)=(1,4)) \\\\  & +P((X,Y)=(1,6))+P((X,Y)=(2,6)) \\\\  & =f(1,2)+f(1,4)+f(1,6)+f(2,6) \\\\  & =0.1+0.25+0.25+0.2 \\\\  & =0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186162293 1068 #edb/ref 17592186158930 13194139533393 17592186045585 false] [17592186162293 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243839 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186162293 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186162293 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}={\\lambda }_{3}=-3.$ Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186162293 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues $λ_{1} = λ_{2} = λ_{3} =-3$. Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." 13194139533393 17592186045585 false] [17592186162293 1074 "Notice that we are given a non-scalar $3\\times 3$ matrix that has the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of algebraic multiplicity $3.$ \n\nAs a result, there are two possibilities for the Jordan blocks corresponding to $\\lambda =-3:$\n\n- *Case I.* We may get the single $3\\times 3$ Jordan block ${J}_{3}(-3).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-3$ form the following chain:\n\n$$\n{v}_{3}\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{2}\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0\n$$\n\n- *Case II.* We may get a composition of one $2\\times 2$ and one $1\\times 1$ Jordan blocks, say ${J}_{2}(-3)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-3).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-3$ form the following chains:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {v}_{3}\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo figure out which of the cases we obtain in this situation, let's find the number of linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda =-3.$\n\nComputing $A+3I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+3I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce this matrix to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+3I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\text{nullity}(A+3I)=1,$ there will be only one eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3,$ say, ${v}_{1}.$ So, the generalized eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n{v}_{3}\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{2}\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have *case I*, and $A$ has the following Jordan canonical form:\n\n$$\n{J}_{3}(-3)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186166757 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243944 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186166757 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243945 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186166757 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243947 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186166758 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243952 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186166759 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243960 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186166759 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243969 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186166857 1038 #edb/ref 17592186244173 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186166857 1038 #edb/ref 17592186244174 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186166857 1038 #edb/ref 17592186244175 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186166857 1038 #edb/ref 17592186244176 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186166857 1038 #edb/ref 17592186244177 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186166857 1038 #edb/ref 17592186244179 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186166858 1038 #edb/ref 17592186244182 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186166858 1038 #edb/ref 17592186244183 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186166858 1038 #edb/ref 17592186244186 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186166858 1038 #edb/ref 17592186244187 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186166858 1038 #edb/ref 17592186244189 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186167067 1068 #edb/ref 17592186163450 13194139533393 17592186045585 false] [17592186167067 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243878 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186167067 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186167067 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = e^{x + 2y + 5}$.\nUsing a substitution of the form $z = x + 2y$, transform the differential equation into an equation of the form $z^{′}(x) = f(z)$. What is the function $f(z)$?" 13194139533393 17592186045585 false] [17592186167067 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x+2y+5}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=x+2y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186167067 1074 "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+2y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{2} & ={e}^{z+5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2{e}^{z+5}+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=2{e}^{z+5}+1.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186167068 1068 #edb/ref 17592186163456 13194139533393 17592186045585 false] [17592186167068 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243850 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186167068 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186167068 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{y+3x+2}-3.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186167068 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\sqrt{y + 3x + 2} - 3$.\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n$g(x, y) = x + C$,\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x, y)$?" 13194139533393 17592186045585 false] [17592186167068 1074 "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=y+3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=3+\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-3 & =\\sqrt{z+2}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{z+2}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z+2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int (z+2)^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 2(z+2)^{1/2} & =x+C \\\\ 2\\sqrt{z+2} & =x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{y+3x+2} & =x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y)=2\\sqrt{y+3x+2}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186167069 1068 #edb/ref 17592186163462 13194139533393 17592186045585 false] [17592186167069 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243479 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186167069 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186167069 1071 "The second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+2y=0,\\,y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=3\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(1)=p \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=f(t),\\, & {x}_{2}(1)=q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t),$ and $p$ and $q$ are constants. Calculate  $(p-q)\\cdot f(t).$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186167069 1071 "The second-order initial value problem\n$y^{″} - 3y^{′} + 2y = 0, y(1) = 2, y^{′}(1) = 3$\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n${x_{1}^{′} = x_{2},\\begin{vmatrix}x_{1}(1) = p \\\\ x_{2}^{′} = f(t),\\end{vmatrix}x_{2}(1) = q$\nwhere $x_{1}(t) = y(t)$, $x_{2}(t) = y^{′}(t)$, and $p$ and $q$ are constants. Calculate $(p - q) \\cdot f(t)$." 13194139533393 17592186045585 false] [17592186167069 1074 "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also convert the initial conditions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & ={x}_{1}(1)=2 \\\\ {y}^{′}(1) & ={x}_{2}(1)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=3{y}^{′}-2y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=3{x}_{2}-2{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(1)=2 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=-2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{2}(1)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $p=2,q=3,f(t)=-2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2},$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p-q)\\cdot f(t) & =(2-3)f(t) \\\\  & =-f(t) \\\\  & =2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186167074 1068 #edb/ref 17592186163492 13194139533393 17592186045585 false] [17592186167074 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243719 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186167074 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186167074 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $xm$, its velocity is $vm/s$ and its acceleration is $4xm/s^{2}$. If $P$ has a displacement of $2m$ when its velocity is $8m/s$, find $v$ in terms of $x$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533393 17592186045585 false] [17592186167074 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $4x\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $2\\,m$ when its velocity is $8\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186167074 1074 "![](images/01/017463f3ac8abac9c2b00c4d9a715a4109a5f4bc1b070e0a12c4d3a7bac64351.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $4x\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4x.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=4x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 4x\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=2{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=2\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=8\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(8)^{2} & =2(2)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =32-8 \\\\ C & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2{x}^{2}+24 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4{x}^{2}+48 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4({x}^{2}+12) \\\\ v & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+12}.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186167077 1068 #edb/ref 17592186163510 13194139533393 17592186045585 false] [17592186167077 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243737 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186167077 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186167077 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x > 0$. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $vm/s$ and the magnitude of its acceleration is $6x^{3}m/s^{2}$. Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O$, which differential equation describes t