6229 1071 "![](images/d0/d0964ff8219d84181163537f5660fe2b504668b1e97e3461cc8cb43d18fc6091.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral\n$∮_(C)(3y - e^{\\cos x})dx + (7x + \\sqrt{y + 5})dy$?" 13194139533393 17592186045585 false] [17592186176229 1071 "![](images/d0/d0964ff8219d84181163537f5660fe2b504668b1e97e3461cc8cb43d18fc6091.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}(3y-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\,dx+(7x+\\sqrt{y+5})\\,dy\\,?\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186176229 1074 "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/de/deeb5648e328ecc93cf6c1de620e103da0f7677705d1e89e4654deff8d411f66.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=3y-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ and $Q(x,y)=7x+\\sqrt{y+5},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(3y-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\,dx+(7x+\\sqrt{y+5})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(7-3)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}4\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}4r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186176232 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173620 13194139533393 17592186045585 false] [17592186176232 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243489 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186176232 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186176232 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & a \\\\ 1 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $a-2b.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186176232 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}, J = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}, P = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & a \\\\ 1 & b\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A = PJP^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A$, find the value of $a - 2b$." 13194139533393 17592186045585 false] [17592186176232 1074 "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{2}$ such that $(A-3I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}.$\n\nNow, computing $A-3I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]-(3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A-3I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-1+2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-2b=1-2\\cdot 1=-1.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186176236 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173644 13194139533393 17592186045585 false] [17592186176236 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243444 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186176236 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186176236 1071 "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533393 17592186045585 false] [17592186176236 1071 "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186176236 1074 "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-8-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-8-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-5\\cdot (-5) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(-3)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186176240 106