ligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{1}{-2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186176241 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173674 13194139533393 17592186045585 false] [17592186176241 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243614 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186176241 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186176241 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & a \\\\ 1 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $a-2b.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186176241 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, J = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, P = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & a \\\\ 1 & b\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A = PJP^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A$, find the value of $a - 2b$." 13194139533393 17592186045585 false] [17592186176241 1074 "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{2}$ such that $(A-I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}.$\n\nNow, computing $A-I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A-I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=1+2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-2b=3-2\\cdot 1=1.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186176244 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173692 13194139533393 17592186045585 false] [17592186176244 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243683 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186176244 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186176244 1071 "Consider the arc of the curve $y = \\frac{2}{3}x^{3/2}$ that lies between the lines $x = 0$ and $x = 3$. Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2π$ radians about the $y$-axis." 13194139533393 17592186045585 false] [17592186176244 1071 "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=3.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186176244 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2})={x}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}=x.\n$$\n\nThen, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\sqrt{1+x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=1+x.$ Then, $\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.$\n\nWe also need to compute new limits:\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\sqrt{1+x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}(u-1)\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}({u}^{3/2}-{u}^{1/2})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\pi {[\\frac{2{u}^{5/2}}{5}-\\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =2\\pi [\\frac{2(4)^{5/2}}{5}-\\frac{2(4)^{3/2}}{3}-(\\frac{2(1)^{5/2}}{5}-\\frac{2(1)^{3/2}}{3})] \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{64}{5}-\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{116}{15}) \\\\  & =\\frac{232\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186184173 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243990 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186184173 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243991 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186184202 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243964 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186233107 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243696 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186233107 1071 "Express $z=\\frac{6}{-1+\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ).$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 false] [17592186233107 1071 "Express $z=\\frac{6}{-1+\\text{i}}$ in the form $r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186233107 1074 "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $-1-\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{6}{-1+\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-1-\\text{i})}{(-1+\\text{i})(-1-\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-1-\\text{i})}{(-1)^{2}-{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-1-\\text{i})}{1+1} \\\\  & =3(-1-\\text{i}) \\\\  & =-3-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn polar form, $r$ represents the magnitude $|z|.$ For a complex number $z=x+y\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, for $z=-3-3\\text{i},$ the magnitude is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn polar form, $\\theta $  represents the argument $\\operatorname{arg}(z).$ Since $x=-3$ and $y=-3$ are both negative, $z$ lies in the third quadrant. So, we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}(z) & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}|\\frac{-3}{-3}| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}1 \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the complex number $z$ in polar form:\n\n$$\nz=3\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}))\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 false] [17592186233107 1074 "First, we express the given complex number in the form $z=x+y\\text{i}.$ To do this, we remove $\\text{i}$ from the denominator by multiplying the numerator and denominator of $z$ by the complex conjugate of the denominator, which is $-1-\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{6}{-1+\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-1-\\text{i})}{(-1+\\text{i})(-1-\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\fra