92186241479 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243890 1075 #edb/ref 17592186241640 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243890 1075 #edb/ref 17592186241823 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243890 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242022 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243890 1075 #edb/ref 17592186243347 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243890 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243890 1077 "selection" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243890 1079 #edb/ref 17592186242022 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243890 1080 #uuid "ff9c9993-a272-54fc-b7cd-ff51bde6faba" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243891 1075 #edb/ref 17592186241263 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243891 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242164 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243891 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242272 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243891 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242389 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243891 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242439 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243891 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243891 1077 "selection" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243891 1079 #edb/ref 17592186242164 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243891 1080 #uuid "ffc26d75-faf8-505d-a291-cd32997718d5" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243892 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243815 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243892 1069 "q-106464" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243892 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243892 1071 "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y-5)^{2}=2(x-2)?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243892 1072 #uuid "ab3c3b80-a498-5921-8e28-4f84e8be9a6a" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243892 1074 "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h)$ that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),$ we see that the parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(2,5).$ So we have\n\n$$\nx-2=p{t}^{2},\\,y-5=2pt.\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the right-hand sides of the given Cartesian equation and the general case, then solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p(x-h) & =2(x-2) \\\\ 4p(x-2) & =2(x-2) \\\\ 4p & =2 \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-2=(\\frac{1}{2}){t}^{2},\\,y-5=2(\\frac{1}{2})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y-5=t.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243893 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243665 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243893 1069 "q-106465" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243893 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243893 1071 "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y+1)^{2}=-(x-3)?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243893 1072 #uuid "c41f08e4-aabd-53ce-9553-aaaeafb4f1d1" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243893 1074 "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h)$ that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),$ we see that the parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(3,-1).$ So we have\n\n$$\nx-3=p{t}^{2},\\,y+1=2pt.\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the right-hand sides of the given Cartesian equation and the general case, then solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p(x-h) & =-(x-3) \\\\ 4p(x-3) & =-(x-3) \\\\ 4p & =-1 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-3=(-\\frac{1}{4}){t}^{2},\\,y+1=2(-\\frac{1}{4})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-3=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{2}t.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243894 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243871 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243894 1069 "q-106473" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243894 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243894 1071 "Consider the equation ${x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}+26{x}^{2}+50x+25=0.$ Given that $x=-1$ is a solution of multiplicity $2,$ find the remaining solutions." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243894 1072 #uuid "8f672d1c-b85f-5ff9-ae17-415d8bf1e89b" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243894 1074 "Let $f(x)={x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}+26{x}^{2}+50x+25.$ Since ${x}_{1,2}=-1$ is a root of multiplicity $2,$ according to the factor theorem, \n\n$$\n(x+1)^{2}={x}^{2}+2x+1\n$$\n\nis a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nNow, we can use long division to find the quotient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}+25 \\\\  & {x}^{2}+2x+1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}+26{x}^{2}+50x+25\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,25{x}^{2}+50x+25 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{25{x}^{2}+50x+25}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}+2x+1)({x}^{2}+25).$\n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve ${x}^{2}+25=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+25 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-25 \\\\ x & =\\pm 5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $x=-1,\\,-1,\\,\\pm 5\\text{i}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243895 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243647 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243895 1069 "q-106479" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243895 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243895 1071 "Find all of the solutions to ${x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+14{x}^{2}-8x+40=0$ given that $x=1+3\\text{i}$ is a solution." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243895 1072 #uuid "02a25eeb-894f-55d6-b223-5c0a7fe16478" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243895 1074 "First, we let $f(x)={x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+14{x}^{2}-8x+40.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=1+3\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{1+3\\text{i}}=1-3\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$(x-(1+3\\text{i}))(x-(1-3\\text{i}))$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(1+3\\text{i}))(x-(1-3\\text{i})) & ={x}^{2}-(1+3\\text{i})x-(1-3\\text{i})x+(1+3\\text{i})(1-3\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+({1}^{2}-(3\\text{i})^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}-2x+10$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}+4 \\\\  & {x}^{2}-2x+10\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+14{x}^{2}-8x+40\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\,\\,{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+10{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,4{x}^{2}-8x+40 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\,\\,\\,\\,4{x}^{2}-8x+40}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}-2x+10)({x}^{2}+4).$\n\nTo find the other two roots, we solve ${x}^{2}+4=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots of our quartic equation are $x=1\\pm 3\\text{i},\\,\\pm 2\\text{i}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243896 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243473 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243896 1069 "q-1065" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243896 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243896 1071 "What is the augmented matrix for the following system of linear equations?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}9x+3y-z=16 \\\\ 15-8x=-4y \\\\ -y+20x=3z\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243896 1072 #uuid "22e9b9ea-bb31-5721-a113-98ad40800ad3" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243896 1074 "First, we organize the system so that the variables are ordered $x,y,z$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}9x+3y-z=16 \\\\ -8x+4y+0z=-15 \\\\ 20x-y-3z=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}9 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -8 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 20 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe augmented matrix  is the coefficient matrix augmented with the column that represents the right-hand sides of the equations:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}9 & 3 & -1 & 16 \\\\ -8 & 4 & 0 & -15 \\\\ 20 & -1 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243897 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243569 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243897 1069 "q-106500" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243897 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243897 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx+3=4t,\\,y+5=2{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243897 1072 #uuid "f0a0ce4f-a9db-5835-9fcb-de8fc238b8d3" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243897 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{x+3}{4},\\,{t}^{2}=\\frac{y+5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{x+3}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{y+5}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{y+5}{2} \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =8(y+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243898 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243818 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243898 1069 "q-106502" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243898 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243898 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-2=-4t,\\,y-5=-2{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243898 1072 #uuid "cc14080e-5154-5a78-ad65-c0fdceff6c7a" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243898 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-\\frac{x-2}{4},\\,{t}^{2}=-\\frac{y-5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{x-2}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{y-5}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16} & =-\\frac{y-5}{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =-8(y-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243899 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243539 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243899 1069 "q-106503" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243899 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243899 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx+2=-\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,y+3=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243899 1072 #uuid "2eccd894-3046-5446-8f74-99889a565496" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243899 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-2(x+2),\\,{t}^{2}=-4(y+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-2(x+2))}^{2} \\\\  & =4{(x+2)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-4(y+3)$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x+2)}^{2} & =-4(y+3) \\\\ (x+2)^{2} & =-(y+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243900 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243613 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243900 1069 "q-106505" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243900 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243900 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx+3=-3{t}^{2},\\,y+5=-6t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243900 1072 #uuid "06fba2ee-cba4-5050-b5e0-04beeb35b6b6" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243900 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-\\frac{x+3}{3},\\,t=-\\frac{y+5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{y+5}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{x+3}{3}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{36} & =-\\frac{x+3}{3} \\\\ (y+5)^{2} & =36\\cdot (-\\frac{x+3}{3}) \\\\ (y+5)^{2} & =-12(x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243901 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243901 1069 "q-106509" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243901 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243901 1071 "What are the parametric equations of the hyperbola ${x}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243901 1072 #uuid "c2382b48-3dd4-5afd-aecb-01a938877297" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243901 1074 "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=1$ and $b=\\sqrt{9}=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243902 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243758 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243902 1069 "q-106515" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243902 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243902 1071 "![](images/99/999d1d14a31f9619306efe6b1c2ebc6e900a52bf4e2d2edd7230e7dd7c9b25b9.png)\n\nFind the parametric equations of the hyperbola shown above given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm 2x.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243902 1072 #uuid "ae1694d4-eba5-5fa3-9a20-43281e4e1e4e" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243902 1074 "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(\\pm a,0).$ From the graph, we have that $(1,0)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=1.$\n\nThe asymptotes of a horizontal hyperbola are $y=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are $y=\\pm 2x,$ and so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{b}{a} & =\\frac{2}{1} \\\\ \\frac{b}{1} & =\\frac{2}{1} \\\\ b & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243903 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243867 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243903 1069 "q-106517" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243903 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243903 1071 "![](images/b5/b570ce72a87d7583a230ea1c863656f982c7fefeb546295d41429876cc823837.png)\n\nFind the parametric equations of the hyperbola shown above given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{3}{2}x.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243903 1072 #uuid "020ddd15-37b6-5806-8af7-4e0809b58383" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243903 1074 "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(\\pm a,0).$ From the graph, we have that $(2,0)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=2.$\n\nThe asymptotes of a horizontal hyperbola are $y=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{3}{2}x,$ and so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{b}{a} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{b}{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ b & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243904 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243561 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243904 1069 "q-106521" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243904 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243904 1071 "![](images/2f/2fe325dcc61b0cec5483791e00842ca975b5d712ffed93cf48eacc52a6cd8daf.png)\n\nFind the parametric equations of the hyperbola shown above given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}x.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243904 1072 #uuid "6b8a480e-d5fc-5495-8619-7a1fa0b2b32d" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243904 1074 "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(\\pm a,0).$ From the graph, we have that $(\\sqrt{2},0)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nThe asymptotes of a horizontal hyperbola are $y=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}x,$ and so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{b}{a} & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{b}{\\sqrt{2}} & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ b & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243905 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243679 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243905 1069 "q-106522" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243905 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243905 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243905 1072 #uuid "7348e9e6-dfba-5dbc-a5b3-afaa8281be9c" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243905 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{3} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243906 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243784 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243906 1069 "q-106524" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243906 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243906 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243906 1072 #uuid "af943c2b-8efb-52a1-9ba0-375300448462" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243906 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{3}} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{12} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{12} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243907 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243881 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243907 1069 "q-106526" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243907 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243907 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243907 1072 #uuid "15e0fdb8-5dad-558c-a81b-b6d856e0082f" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243907 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{3} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{5}} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{5} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{5} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243908 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243502 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243908 1069 "q-106527" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243908 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243908 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243908 1072 #uuid "dbb3616e-4e51-5e30-89c0-04fbeeb5001e" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243908 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{3\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(3\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{18} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{3}} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{3} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{18}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243909 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243580 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243909 1069 "q-106528" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243909 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243909 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243909 1072 #uuid "14ba5bcb-d69f-50c5-9b72-1cf9a802a574" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243909 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{5} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(5)^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{3}} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{12} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{12}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{12} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243910 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243780 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243910 1069 "q-106594" 13194139533393 1759218604