\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-hyperbola, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](images/b6/b696475163cf9627709e62d8315edca393afd46aa2dbff5f23b4583e20641864.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0],$ we get the quarter-hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe the required quarter-hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0].\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243912 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243686 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243912 1069 "q-106598" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243912 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243912 1071 "![](images/b8/b876f823db1cc3be83a0ae415d0101b540527e83c818d288aa9a812d70740a99.png)\n\nThe quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243912 1072 #uuid "6b590f9f-ca02-50b0-8c0a-677bf4238ea8" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243912 1074 "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-hyperbola, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](images/d8/d8f735790daf267a094ecd8c336d90de65d84e3985485213eb541364bd14aacd.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ],$ we get only the quarter-hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe the required quarter-hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ].\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243913 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243770 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243913 1069 "q-106608" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243913 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243913 1071 "What are the parametric equations of the hyperbola $5{y}^{2}-4{x}^{2}=20?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243913 1072 #uuid "48ac7252-bac8-52c8-8ed8-fe1d4199fccd" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243913 1074 "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation of our hyperbola in standard form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{2}-4{x}^{2} & =20 \\\\ \\frac{5{y}^{2}}{20}-\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{20} & =\\frac{20}{20} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, $a=\\sqrt{4}=2$ and $b=\\sqrt{5}.$ Therefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243914 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243445 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243914 1069 "q-106615" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243914 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243914 1071 "![](images/09/09873582f6b52b28ca5223605479cc98df4ead7cf0940ea4cadfa66301694911.png)\n\nFind the parametric equations of the hyperbola shown above given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}x.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243914 1072 #uuid "2c08da87-7b26-53d8-a4dd-a3ef3d388f9f" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243914 1074 "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\nx=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(0,\\pm a).$ From the graph, we have that $(0,\\sqrt{3})$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nRecall also that the asymptotes of the hyperbola occur at $y=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a}{b} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{b} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ b & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243915 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243790 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243915 1069 "q-106617" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243915 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243915 1071 "![](images/60/6044a47f621612d56138893be089f48a2d2a7060c13d044086154ab7892fac38.png)\n\nFind the parametric equations of the hyperbola shown above given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{1}{2}x.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243915 1072 #uuid "2d68f03b-3725-5bc6-bcce-fd5047b66b02" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243915 1074 "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\nx=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(0,\\pm a).$ From the graph, we have that $(0,1)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=1.$\n\nRecall also that the asymptotes of the hyperbola occur at $y=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{1}{2}x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a}{b} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{b} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ b & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243916 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243526 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243916 1069 "q-106620" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243916 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243916 1071 "![](images/e2/e2203c195dbcd9ae474f5d00f00e81d63ec6527c0367e96a8a7520522c967265.png)\n\nFind the parametric equations of the hyperbola shown above given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}x.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243916 1072 #uuid "ab9f1399-9515-5682-b245-3f82ab7ad6fe" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243916 1074 "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\nx=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(0,\\pm a).$ From the graph, we have that $(0,-3)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=3.$\n\nRecall also that the asymptotes of the hyperbola occur at $y=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a}{b} & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{b} & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ b & =\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243917 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243704 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243917 1069 "q-106623" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243917 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243917 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243917 1072 #uuid "1eee5c4e-0644-59a1-b819-0a637e0aafee" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243917 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent Identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{5}} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{5} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}+1 & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243918 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243584 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243918 1069 "q-106626" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243918 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243918 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243918 1072 #uuid "118e20fb-8b1f-5237-8abf-63e94df22dfc" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243918 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent Identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{3}} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{3} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+1 & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243919 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243498 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243919 1069 "q-106647" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243919 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243919 1071 "Given that $y>9$ and $t>0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243919 1072 #uuid "23774b05-7a15-54bf-bb21-810326ad31c6" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243919 1074 "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-5(y-9).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=5,$ and $a=9.$ Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y>9$ for all $t>0.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243920 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243848 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243920 1069 "q-106651" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243920 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243920 1071 "Given that $y>8$ and $t>0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243920 1072 #uuid "5077cc12-6631-56e6-a777-59697d379c16" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243920 1074 "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-k(y-a),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y>a$ for all $t>0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited decay equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=176-22y.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=-22(y-8).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=22,$ and $a=8.$ Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y>8$ for all $t>0.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243921 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243574 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243921 1069 "q-106671" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243921 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243921 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x=8\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243921 1072 #uuid "8e7a3969-02f2-5af9-9af8-d0d3eea06f1d" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243921 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent Identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =8\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{8} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{8}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{64} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Squaring the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{5} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{64}+1 & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{64} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243922 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243491 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243922 1069 "q-106682" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243922 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243922 1071 "![](images/ab/ab11a3dde0aebbd4088c498e37da48909f9a309ca9d4ab29bfcfa788d53e5c5e.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243922 1072 #uuid "35cbe7c5-ecdf-566f-98f8-b37097adbf40" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243922 1074 "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a single branch, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](images/f8/f825ca9f9431ca38b14ed141639d5cf4189f0fde0526e943ddb956e112c3e4ab.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $[-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ],$ we get only the lower branch.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe only the lower branch are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cup (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ].\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243923 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243777 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243923 1069 "q-106684" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243923 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243923 1071 "Let $L$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices with real entries and determinant $0.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\n- The  zero-matrix $[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $L.$\n- For any $A\\in L,$ we can find the corresponding negative matrix $-A\\in L$ such that $A+(-A)=O.$\n- For any $A,B,C\\in L,$ we have $(A+B)-C=A+(B-C).$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243923 1072 #uuid "0880be83-dbd5-5dc8-a3cc-bdb045c80795" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243923 1074 "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|=0.$ Hence, $[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\in L.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any matrix $A\\in L,$ there is a corresponding negative matrix $-A$ such that\n\n$$\nA+(-A)=A-A=O.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n|-A|=|-1\\cdot A|=(-1)^{2}\\cdot |A|=1\\cdot 0=0,\n$$\n\nhence $-A\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any matrices $A,B\\in L,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n(A+B)-C=(A+B)+(-C)=A+(B+(-C))=A+(B-C).\n$$\n\nsince matrix addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243924 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243718 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243924 1069 "q-106685" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243924 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243924 1071 "![](images/32/32375f839dd0f51a8624bebda732d7413130c940045db7e5a1a9fce3576e91b6.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243924 1072 #uuid "9421f991-1b84-5607-8912-60b61e3c03d4" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243924 1074 "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a single branch, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](images/d4/d44195c764b2943c3d5b5e0d91ee770011c951d7a825f0c5cc4d1f38cedbb709.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we get only the upper branch.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe only the upper branch are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243925 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243670 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243925 1069 "q-106687" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243925 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243925 1071 "![](images/14/1474fb8efcada2aec61c1aa2b2ed16615e0ba968ec4e53a8fbfe05b1107c085f.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243925 1072 #uuid "06b680fc-bd0c-57a1-af6c-a633b194f7da" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243925 1074 "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a single branch, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](images/46/46c9d9139a3cff2a76871c69deeac60492d2c9aef4bf1f7ac07a16868d31d6e0.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we get only the upper branch.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe only the upper branch are\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243926 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243747 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243926 1069 "q-106688" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243926 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243926 1071 "Let $S$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices with real entries and determinant $1.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\n- The identity matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $S.$\n- For any $A\\in S,$ we can find the corresponding negative matrix $-A\\in S$ such that $A+(-A)=O.$\n- There are matrices $A,B,C\\in S,$ such that $(A+B)+C\\ne A+(B+C).$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243926 1072 #uuid "e2555255-85b1-529f-85fb-94b6f9684401" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243926 1074 "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|=1.$ Hence, $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\in S.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any matrix $A\\in S,$ there is a corresponding negative $2\\times 2$ matrix $-A$ such that\n\n$$\nA+(-A)=A-A=O.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n|-A|=|-1\\cdot A|=(-1)^{2}\\cdot |A|=1\\cdot 1=1,\n$$\n\nhence $-A\\in S.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For any matrices $A,B,C\\in S,$ we have\n\n$$\n(A+B)+C=A+(B+C).\n$$\n\nsince matrix addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243927 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243793 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243927 1069 "q-106693" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243927 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243927 1071 "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(5,-3)?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243927 1072 #uuid "4bff5c9a-6bfa-5bc8-99ff-8f60b509a054" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243927 1074 "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(5,-3).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243928 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243838 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243928 1069 "q-106694" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243928 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243928 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{2x-{x}^{5}}{{x}^{3}+5}=$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243928 1072 #uuid "a5bfb77e-165a-5915-a6e7-be81e5ce9578" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243928 1074 "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{2x-{x}^{5}}{{x}^{3}+5} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-{x}^{2})}{(1+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{0-{x}^{2}}{1-0} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,{x}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243929 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243799 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243929 1069 "q-106696" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243929 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243929 1071 "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243929 1072 #uuid "4f054c8b-83ad-51a3-ac06-f9b576f40779" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243929 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & ={(\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={(\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\frac{25}{4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{25}{4}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243930 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243656 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243930 1069 "q-106697" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243930 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243930 1071 "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{7}-3{x}^{2}+4x}{{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+5}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243930 1072 #uuid "e2bbf6bd-a57e-500b-a9a6-06098ef957a5" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243930 1074 "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{4}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{7}-3{x}^{2}+4x}{{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+5} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{7}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{4x}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{({x}^{3}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{({x}^{3}-0+0)}{(1-0+0)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243931 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243567 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243931 1069 "q-106698" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243931 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243931 1071 "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{7{x}^{5}-3{x}^{2}}{6-2x}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243931 1072 #uuid "9190dbc2-1fbd-5733-8a56-c3110e85a822" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243931 1074 "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{7{x}^{5}-3{x}^{2}}{6-2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{7{x}^{5}}{x}-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{x})}{(\\frac{6}{x}-\\frac{2x}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(7{x}^{4}-3x)}{(\\frac{6}{x}-2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(7{x}^{4}-3x)}{0-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(7{x}^{4}-3x) \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243932 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243733 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243932 1069 "q-106700" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243932 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243932 1071 "Let $M$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $[\\begin{aligned}a & 1 \\\\ b & c\\end{aligned}],$ where $a,b,c\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\n- For any $A\\in M,$ we have $-A\\in M$ such that $A+(-A)=0.$\n- For the zero matrix $O$ and any $A\\in M,$ we have $A+O=A.$\n- For any $A,B,C\\in M,$ we have $(A+B)+C=A+(B+C).$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243932 1072 #uuid "6f184570-b064-50c3-a82a-5d76cef8f98e" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243932 1074 "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\in M.$ Then $-A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ is the corresponding negative matrix such that $A+(-A)=O.$ However, $-{a}_{12}=-1\\ne 1,$ hence, $-A\\notin M.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for the zero-matrix $O$ and any matrix $A\\in M,$ we have\n\n$$\nA+O=A\n$$\n\nsince $O$ is the unique zero element in the set of all matrices.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, For any matrices $A,B,C\\in M,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n(A+B)+C=A+(B+C)\n$$\n\nsince matrix addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243933 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243783 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243933 1069 "q-106701" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243933 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243933 1071 "![](images/db/db61966ec9692ab0e21077c616eeb530589e2d8a617891f6c350bc1b5df4351c.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243933 1072 #uuid "f59c2c46-d3e0-513d-b9a2-86449659a748" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243933 1074 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=7$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243934 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243671 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243934 1069 "q-106702" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243934 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243934 1071 "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{11{x}^{5}-9{x}^{6}}{2{x}^{5}-1}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243934 1072 #uuid "ad026744-5a39-53f7-ada7-a9cd94dba36b" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243934 1074 "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{5}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{11{x}^{5}-9{x}^{6}}{2{x}^{5}-1} \\\\  & {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{11{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{9{x}^{6}}{{x}^{5}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(11-9x)}{(2-\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{11-9x}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(11-9x) \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243935 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243731 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243935 1069 "q-106703" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243935 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243935 1071 "Let $T$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients and whose degree is at most $10.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- For the zero-polynomial $0$ and any $p(x)\\in T,$ we have $p(x)+0=0.$\n- For any $p(x),q(x)\\in T$, we have $p(x)+q(x)=p(x)-q(x).$\n- For any polynomial $p(x)\\in T$ we have $-p(x)\\in T$ such that $p(x)+(-p(x))=0.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243935 1072 #uuid "c35f03a5-0abf-5f24-a015-92c06eed08bd" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243935 1074 "Let's examine our statements one at a time.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, consider the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}$ in $T.$ Then,\n\n$$\np(x)+0={x}^{2}+0={x}^{2}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider the polynomials $p(x)=q(x)={x}^{2}\\in T.$\nThen:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x)+q(x) & ={x}^{2}+{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2} \\\\ p(x)-q(x) & ={x}^{2}-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $p(x)+q(x)\\ne p(x)-q(x).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any polynomial $p(x)\\in T,$ there is a corresponding negative polynomial $-p(x)$ such that\n\n$$\np(x)+(-p(x))=p(x)-p(x)=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{deg}⁡(-p(x))=\\operatorname{deg}⁡(p(x))\\le 10,\n$$\n\nhence $-p(x)\\in T.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243936 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243804 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243936 1069 "q-106704" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243936 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243936 1071 "![](images/5b/5bd3bf7cecf806ab0fc89db59afc5d385064080f47076d13d2ef25650ce39a23.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243936 1072 #uuid "59b706b1-e2ca-5d92-9b24-fa27cdd71036" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243936 1074 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(3,-2),$ $a=3$ and $b=8.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243937 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243624 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243937 1069 "q-106705" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243937 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243937 1071 "What is the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ for $x>0$ with $f(x)=\\frac{|{x}^{3}-5|}{2-{x}^{3}}?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243937 1072 #uuid "1d3780ea-9bd6-553b-af0e-868098039a4e" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243937 1074 "For large positive values of $x,$ we have $|{x}^{3}-5|=({x}^{3}-5).$ Consequently, for $x\\to \\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{|{x}^{3}-5|}{2-{x}^{3}}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}-5}{2-{x}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe horizontal asymptote for $x>0$ is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}-5}{2-{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(1-\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-0}{0-1} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-1.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243938 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243571 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243938 1069 "q-106710" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243938 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243938 1071 "Let $C$ be the set of all polynomials $p(x)$ such that $p(0)<0.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The zero-polynomial belongs to $C.$\n- For any $p(x),q(x)\\in C,$ we have $p(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x).$\n- The addition of polynomials in $C$ is associative." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243938 1072 #uuid "7c34661b-de46-5e42-88de-7fe1ed369bf4" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243938 1074 "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $C$ contains only polynomials that evaluate to a negative number at $0.$ However, the zero-polynomial is *not* such a polynomial, as it equals $0≮0$ at $0.$ So, $0\\notin C.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in C,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\np(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x)\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is commutative.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x),q(x),r(x)\\in C,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x)=p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)]\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243939 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243608 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243939 1069 "q-106711" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243939 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243939 1071 "![](images/84/8498bb189b51954a79f8ca6638631d6acd0a3d4fe86f6f4cecb2de77ee370f94.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243939 1072 #uuid "8c82fb7c-60f3-5ec6-bb6a-1161e86d2f27" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243939 1074 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=4$ and $b=2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/fb/fb686f7295c2c6ac3058361ecc0438ae56d650950833185b17ec959d654cc511.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ].\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243940 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243520 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243940 1069 "q-106715" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243940 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243940 1071 "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243940 1072 #uuid "00eda352-d2e8-5490-8b6e-f1ece02f57de" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243940 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{3} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{5} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243941 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243591 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243941 1069 "q-106718" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243941 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243941 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{(x-2)^{3}}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243941 1072 #uuid "9125f4a8-c018-5be2-a3e3-206bf77539d3" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243941 1074 "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since $(x-2)^{3}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{(x-2)^{3}}=0.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243942 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243530 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243942 1069 "q-106727" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243942 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243942 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2-{x}^{2}}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243942 1072 #uuid "9ec04ce8-c2fc-55aa-9357-bfe8bb93a396" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243942 1074 "First, we rewrite the limit as\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2-{x}^{2}}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}-2}.\n$$\n\nThe numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with\n\n$$\n|-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|\\le 3.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator ${x}^{2}-2$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ So the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2-{x}^{2}}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}-2}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2-{x}^{2}},$ shown below:\n\n![](images/9f/9fade66030e2e416f92138d787999d3ca3a7dd4788ad39cf29dd07ff944038da.png)" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243943 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243861 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243943 1069 "q-106729" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243943 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243943 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2x-{x}^{4}}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243943 1072 #uuid "1e04a7d7-dd6e-5c5c-9b09-c1a9602d3772" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243943 1074 "First, we rewrite the limit as\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2x-{x}^{4}}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{4}-2x}.\n$$\n\nThe numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with\n\n$$\n|-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|\\le 5.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator ${x}^{4}-2x$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ So the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2x-{x}^{4}}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{4}-2x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2x-{x}^{4}},$ shown below:\n\n![](images/08/0865e078246952abcfcd570887a38edfb8120066cd6ee9ad54edc61bac5ddc52.png)" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243944 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243781 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243944 1069 "q-106731" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243944 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243944 1071 "$$\n{A}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Find the matrix $C$ given that $B={{A}_{1},{A}_{2}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{A}_{1},{A}_{2}},$ and the coordinates of the matrix $C$ relative to $B$ are $[C]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243944 1072 #uuid "86779615-b7e9-524a-8c43-851c650510ff" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243944 1074 "Any vector in $\\text{Span}{{A}_{1},{A}_{2}}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{A}_{1}+{x}_{2}{A}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n[C]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =(-2)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]+2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243945 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243855 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243945 1069 "q-106734" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243945 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243945 1071 "Consider the polynomials ${p}_{1}(t)$ and ${p}_{2}(t),$ given by\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2{t}^{2}+{t}^{3},\\,{p}_{2}(t)=1+3{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind $p(t)$ given that $B={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{p}_{1},{p}_{2}},$ and the coordinates of $p(t)$ relative to $B$ are $[p(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243945 1072 #uuid "9c627703-4264-50cf-a9c8-7aa84f6cc3ca" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243945 1074 "Any vector in  $\\text{Span}{{p}_{1},{p}_{2}}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n[p(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(t) & =3\\cdot (2{t}^{2}+{t}^{3})+(-2)\\cdot (1+3{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}+3{t}^{3}-2-6{t}^{2} \\\\  & =-2+3{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243946 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243579 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243946 1069 "q-106736" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243946 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243946 1071 "What is the range of $f(x)=-\\frac{3}{5-2x}-2?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243946 1072 #uuid "dee89207-4330-5745-b12b-28c3a1d92f86" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243946 1074 "A transformed reciprocal function of the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{a}{bx+c}+d\n$$\n\nhas the horizontal asymptote $y=d,$ and therefore, its range is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,d)\\cup (d,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $d=-2.$ Therefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-2,$ and the range of the function is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (-2,\\infty ).