fore, its range is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,d)\\cup (d,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $d=-\\frac{5}{2}.$ Therefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-\\frac{5}{2},$ and the range of the function is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{2})\\cup (-\\frac{5}{2},\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe graph $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/be/be93340e6c9bd5cad811cb5c5d879c2ad86fbf70eba1f53546a5f8b2fadbd074.png)" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243949 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243469 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243949 1069 "q-106741" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243949 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243949 1071 "What is the range of $f(x)=\\frac{3}{1-5x}+\\frac{3}{4}?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243949 1072 #uuid "9182dff6-8be6-558a-bd25-83eb9047c15b" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243949 1074 "A transformed reciprocal function of the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{a}{bx+c}+d\n$$\n\nhas the horizontal asymptote $y=d,$ and therefore, its range is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,d)\\cup (d,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $d=\\frac{3}{4}.$ Therefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=\\frac{3}{4},$ and the range of the function is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{4})\\cup (\\frac{3}{4},\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe graph $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/00/00f66fd1de73592b3e0113b56ee23f334c2643b1e55fc185dfa9b9126d6ad680.png)" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243950 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243517 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243950 1069 "q-106744" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243950 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243950 1071 "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243950 1072 #uuid "1e309174-90b1-5dec-b490-77c152128db8" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243950 1074 "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $v$ is an eigenvector, then there must exist a real number $\\lambda $ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ Hence, we have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=\\lambda [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis leads to the following system of equations for $\\lambda :$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1=\\lambda  \\\\ -2=-\\lambda \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system has no solution. Therefore, $v$ is not an eigenvector of $A.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243951 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243627 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243951 1069 "q-106751" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243951 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243951 1071 "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A?$\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243951 1072 #uuid "ae199b8f-96fc-541b-8e00-25e26e1c8f40" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243951 1074 "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nA=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as its main diagonal contains a number not equal to $1.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243952 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243524 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243952 1069 "q-106754" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243952 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243952 1071 "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{B}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 20 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf $B={{B}_{1},{B}_{2}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}},$ and $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243952 1072 #uuid "26318539-0a92-5f1e-bd5d-2a9d71f1f3f5" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243952 1074 "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 20 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} & -2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 20 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=4 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=20 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 20 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 20 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=4 \\\\ 7{x}_{2}=14 \\\\ {x}_{2}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=2.$ From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-2\\cdot 2 & =4 \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & =8 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=4+2=6.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243953 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243452 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243953 1069 "q-106756" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243953 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243953 1071 "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A?$\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243953 1072 #uuid "02d764cc-1d89-57be-9543-a3cb1c6ba444" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243953 1074 "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nA=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as its main diagonal contains a number not equal to $1.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The third product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243954 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243846 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243954 1069 "q-106762" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243954 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243954 1071 "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 15 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243954 1072 #uuid "3e43d37a-2ce4-531c-9855-25d3d30a5758" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243954 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 15 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-5,{u}_{22}=6,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=-30.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243955 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243582 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243955 1069 "q-106763" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243955 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243955 1071 "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -7 \\\\ -12 & 18\\end{aligned}]$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243955 1072 #uuid "bd4d8bf8-4a33-530d-bc80-05a910fd04f1" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243955 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -7 \\\\ -12 & 18\\end{aligned}] & \\,{R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243956 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243810 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243956 1069 "q-106765" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243956 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243956 1071 "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 \\\\ 14 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243956 1072 #uuid "4c2a663c-d7cd-55d2-8a80-b4c4edbb70bb" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243956 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 \\\\ 14 & -5\\end{aligned}] & \\,{R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243957 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243765 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243957 1069 "q-106767" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243957 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243957 1071 "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 9 \\\\ -4 & -18\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243957 1072 #uuid "e9e0c6b4-44f3-5a6a-94a9-850df140ef69" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243957 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 9 \\\\ -4 & -18\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-2,$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-2.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243958 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243512 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243958 1069 "q-106769" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243958 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243958 1071 "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 12 & -3\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243958 1072 #uuid "9966c61d-ab71-591b-90f6-3c48ad1407ef" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243958 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 12 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=3,$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=3.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243959 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243474 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243959 1069 "q-106773" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243959 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243959 1071 "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243959 1072 #uuid "096e63c0-3ec3-55a0-bdd9-795c7447a69e" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243959 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-\\frac{2}{3},$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-\\frac{2}{3}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243960 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243845 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243960 1069 "q-106781" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243960 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243960 1071 "Consider the following polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2+{t}^{2},\\,{p}_{2}(t)=1-t+2{t}^{2},\\,q(t)=6+2t.\n$$\n\nIf $B={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)},$ and $[q(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243960 1072 #uuid "5dfc56d2-f4d4-58d9-9174-ebcc9a95025f" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243960 1074 "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)=q(t)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(2+{t}^{2})+{x}_{2}(1-t+2{t}^{2}) & =6+2t \\\\ (2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2})-{x}_{2}t+({x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}){t}^{2} & =6+2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $t$, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ -{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ {x}_{2}=-2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations we get ${x}_{2}=-2.$  Substituting this into the first equation yields\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}+(-2)=6\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[q(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=4+(-2)=2.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243961 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243772 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243961 1069 "q-106809" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243961 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243961 1071 "Let $N$ be the set of all functions of the form $f(x)=a|x|,$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- For any $f(x)\\in N,$ we have $(-1)\\cdot f(x)=f(x).$\n- For any $f(x)\\in N$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).$\n- For any $f(x),g(x)\\in N$ and $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243961 1072 #uuid "d83ac51c-0c75-5956-b55e-b6c1972a1661" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243961 1074 "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, consider $f(x)=|x|\\in N.$ Then,\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot f(x)=(-1)\\cdot |x|=-|x|\\ne |x|.\n$$\n\nHence, $(-1)\\cdot f(x)\\ne f(x).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any function $f(x)\\in N$ and any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any functions $f(x),g(x)\\in N$ and any fixed $x\\in R,$ the results of $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243962 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243750 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243962 1069 "q-106810" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243962 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243962 1071 "Let $J$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients and whose degree is at most $2.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\frac{1}{x}\\in J$\n- For any $p(x)\\in J,$ we have $1\\cdot p(x)\\in J$\n- For any $p(x),q(x)\\in J$ and $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\alpha (p(x)-q(x))=\\alpha p(x)-\\alpha q(x)$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243962 1072 #uuid "253cf041-a7dc-53ad-a439-778e84d95095" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243962 1074 "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $J$ contains only polynomials whose degree is at most $2.$ However, $\\frac{1}{x}$ is *not* a polynomial. So, $\\frac{1}{x}\\notin J.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in J$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot p(x)=p(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $p(x),q(x)\\in J,$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the results of $p(x)$ and $q(x)$ are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (p(x)-q(x))=\\alpha p(x)-\\alpha q(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243963 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243609 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243963 1069 "q-106812" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243963 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243963 1071 "Let $G$ be the set of functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)>0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- For any $f(x)\\in G,$ we have $1\\cdot f(x)=f(x).$\n- For any $f(x)\\in G$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R$, we have $(\\alpha -\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)-\\beta f(x).$\n- The scalar multiplication of functions in $G$ is associative." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243963 1072 #uuid "43cc36f0-0278-53e6-b346-3f4ef69a0aaa" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243963 1074 "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in G$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus,\n\n$$\n(1\\cdot f)(x)=1\\cdot f(x)=f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in G$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha -\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)-\\beta f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in G$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta f(x))=(\\alpha \\beta )f(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243964 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243554 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243964 1069 "q-106813" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243964 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243964 1071 "Let $F$ be the set of functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$F$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243964 1072 #uuid "fe3a1a04-348a-505c-9183-5288567e7407" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243964 1074 "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$F$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because **$F$ does not contain the zero element.** *\n\nIndeed, evaluating the zero function $f(x)=0$ at $x=0,$ we have $f(0)=0\\ne \\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- Addition of $f,g\\in F$ is associative. $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of $f,g\\in F$ is commutative. $\\,✓$\n\n- $1\\cdot f=f$ for all $f\\in F.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition and scalar multiplication of functions satisfy the distributive laws. $\\,✓$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243965 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243602 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243965 1069 "q-106828" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243965 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243965 1071 "The eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ -k & -1\\end{aligned}]$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =k-1$ is  $[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $k.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243965 1072 #uuid "3de44d15-e3e4-5979-86e6-277d8cce23b3" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243965 1074 "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ -k & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] & =(k-1)[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-16 \\\\ -4k+4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4k-4 \\\\ -4k+4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-16=4k-4, \\\\ -4k+4=-4k+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $k$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-12=4k\\,⟹\\,k=-3.\n$$\n\nThe second equation is true for any value of $k.$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $k=-3.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243966 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243825 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243966 1069 "q-106830" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243966 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243966 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3-2x}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243966 1072 #uuid "cfa56d21-de83-52ed-897a-8430dcba3cd1" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243966 1074 "First, we rewrite the limit as\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3-2x}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2x-3}.\n$$\n\nThe numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with\n\n$$\n|-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|\\le 5.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator $2x-3$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ So the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3-2x}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2x-3}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3-2x},$ shown below:\n\n![](images/ab/ab3f7928be50c25689a99751cd180e585d749bf0a5c253c8662f9b820bbf4bb7.png)" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243967 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243594 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243967 1069 "q-106831" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243967 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243967 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1-4x}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243967 1072 #uuid "c9aa0c27-c5d7-570e-9d29-db74ed5fc704" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243967 1074 "First, we rewrite the limit as\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1-4x}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{9\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{4x-1}.\n$$\n\nThe numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with\n\n$$\n|9\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|\\le 9.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator $4x-1$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ So the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1-4x}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{9\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{4x-1}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1-4x},$ shown below:\n\n![](images/64/64700491874195711ad7291ce0471561df307bd099b5b8a38c33269cf08f21ab.png)" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243968 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243708 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243968 1069 "q-106832" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243968 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243968 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2x-{x}^{3}}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243968 1072 #uuid "cc0ba3e8-4087-5985-a70e-9111cce5a30e" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243968 1074 "First, we rewrite the limit as\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2x-{x}^{3}}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{3}-2x}.\n$$\n\nThe numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with\n\n$$\n|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|\\le 1.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator ${x}^{3}-2x$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ So the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2x-{x}^{3}}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{3}-2x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2x-{x}^{3}},$ shown below:\n\n![](images/a9/a9d96a5a85181cddc6eb1bd5f79d6a0238ef16f060ddebf2dc4c48ef1911604f.png)" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243969 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243728 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243969 1069 "q-106835" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243969 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243969 1071 "Consider the following polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)={t}^{2}+t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}-2t+4,\\,{p}_{3}(t)={t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}-2,\\,\\,q(t)=2+{t}^{2}+{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nIf $B={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)},$ and $[q(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}=$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243969 1072 #uuid "b5f3de2d-9c27-55e5-a20a-ab731f514d94" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243969 1074 "We need to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)+{x}_{3}{p}_{3}(t)=q(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}({t}^{2}+t)+{x}_{2}({t}^{2}-2t+4)+{x}_{3}({t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}-2) & =2+{t}^{2}+{t}^{3} \\\\ (4{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})+({x}_{1}-2{x}_{2})t+({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}){t}^{2}+{x}_{3}{t}^{3} & =2+{t}^{2}+{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEquating the coefficients of $t$, we obtain \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=2 \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=1 \\\\ {x}_{3}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}↔{R}_{2} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{3}{4}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+2{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=1.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n4{x}_{2}-2\\cdot 1=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the obtained value into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-2\\cdot 1=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[q(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ and therefore ${x}_{1}=2.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243970 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243826 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243970 1069 "q-106836" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243970 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243970 1071 "Given that \n$A=[\\