n invert ${E}_{3},$ ${E}_{2},$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{3}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & ={E}_{3}^{-1}U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}{E}_{3}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}{E}_{3}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-1,$ ${u}_{33}=4,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{33}=-4.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243976 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243779 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243976 1069 "q-106867" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243976 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243976 1071 "What is the dimension of ${R}_{2}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $2?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243976 1072 #uuid "f3a0ead8-bd4e-563c-a9e6-d670755b3260" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243976 1074 "The standard basis of ${R}_{n}[t]$ is\n\n$$\n{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,\\,{t}^{n}}.\n$$\n\nSo, the standard basis of ${R}_{2}[t]$ is \n\n$$\n{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{2}[t])=3.\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243977 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243509 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243977 1069 "q-106868" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243977 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243977 1071 "What is the dimension of ${R}_{24}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $24?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243977 1072 #uuid "7d133675-64d5-50d6-9a87-de7265046742" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243977 1074 "The standard basis of ${R}_{n}[t]$ is\n\n$$\n{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,\\,{t}^{n}}.\n$$\n\nSo, the standard basis of ${R}_{24}[t]$ is \n\n$$\n{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,\\,{t}^{24}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{24}[t])=24+1=25.\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243978 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243721 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243978 1069 "q-106883" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243978 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243978 1071 "A turkey reaches ${145}^{∘}\\text{F}$ in the oven. After removing the turkey from the oven, the turkey's temperature $T(t)$ decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is ${67}^{∘}\\text{F},$ which differential equation could be used to model the temperature of the turkey? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243978 1072 #uuid "3ba4e3cf-6728-56eb-8731-1748c4ffae5c" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243978 1074 "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={67}^{∘}\\text{F},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-67).\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243979 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243843 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243979 1069 "q-106890" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243979 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243979 1071 "![](images/4a/4adc01d460f42f134f2e6c8da1e2fe516c8e6cfb64bcfeb7a5db89cf93f1fc11.png)\n\nThe vector $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $C$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $-4.$ Find the vector $Cv.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243979 1072 #uuid "bfe870e1-0d53-5aea-b526-5bbc780f92f2" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243979 1074 "The eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Cv & =-4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following picture:\n\n![](images/99/99e3776bfa4048703042970a2479a86c749e3e2a547dce966fceb23b671d66f2.png)" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243980 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243847 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243980 1069 "q-106892" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243980 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243980 1071 "![](images/16/1670f48c1bd70b2c5ddee70c4a9c893edf5e844febd7128b1ddf7773aef0f73f.png)\n\nThe vector $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $C$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $-1.$ Find the vector $Cv.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243980 1072 #uuid "7dc70f42-9e55-5765-97e6-70f88416b22a" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243980 1074 "The eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Cv & =-1v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following picture:\n\n![](images/2e/2e9b0004ae889fb63f5ae65bd2a79915b6e195935fd3b610d2f9957d10f6a9e7.png)" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243981 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243590 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243981 1069 "q-106897" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243981 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243981 1071 "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $10?$\n\n- ${R}_{10}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $10$\n- ${\\text{M}}_{5}(R)$, the vector space of all $5\\times 5$ matrices over $R$\n- ${R}^{10}$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243981 1072 #uuid "4c2f74bb-ddf4-5d4c-b244-ad5b6a694598" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243981 1074 "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{10}[t])=10+1=11\\ne 10.\\,\\times \n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n}(\\text{R}))={n}^{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{5}(R))={5}^{2}=25\\ne 10.\\,\\times \n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}^{n})=n.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{10})=10.\\,✓\n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243982 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243640 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243982 1069 "q-106900" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243982 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243982 1071 "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $3?$\n\n- ${R}^{2}$\n- ${R}_{2}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $2$\n- ${\\text{M}}_{3}(R),$ the vector space of all $3\\times 3$ matrices over $R$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243982 1072 #uuid "bf92f8df-b777-508f-9358-1b7029534cba" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243982 1074 "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}^{n})=n.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{2})=2\\ne 3.\\,\\times \n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{2}[t])=2+1=3.\\,✓\n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n}(R))={n}^{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{3}(R))={3}^{2}=9\\ne 3.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243983 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243798 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243983 1069 "q-106901" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243983 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243983 1071 "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $20?$\n\n- ${R}_{20}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $20$\n- ${\\text{M}}_{4\\times 5}(R),$ the vector space of all $4\\times 5$ matrices over $R$\n- ${R}^{20}$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243983 1072 #uuid "19d4d0ee-ad5b-53e7-956e-b02eb6cf177b" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243983 1074 "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{20}[t])=20+1=21\\ne 20.\\,\\times \n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{4\\times 5}(R))=4\\cdot 5=20.\\,✓\n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}^{n})=n.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{20})=20.\\,✓\n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243984 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243532 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243984 1069 "q-106902" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243984 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243984 1071 "The eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}k & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2k\\end{aligned}]$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =k+2$ is  $[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ k-1\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $k.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243984 1072 #uuid "327cb7ee-35f9-5f98-b530-dbab7d3417be" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243984 1074 "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}k & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ k-1\\end{aligned}] & =(k+2)[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ k-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2k+1 \\\\ -2+2{k}^{2}-2k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-k-2 \\\\ {k}^{2}+k-2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2k+1=-k-2, \\\\ -2+2{k}^{2}-2k={k}^{2}+k-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $k$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-k=-3\\,⟹\\,k=3.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $k=3$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2+2{k}^{2}-2k & ={k}^{2}+k-2 \\\\ -2+2\\cdot (3)^{2}-2\\cdot 3 & =(3)^{2}+3-2 \\\\ 10 & =10,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $k=3.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243985 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243698 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243985 1069 "q-106916" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243985 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243985 1071 "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\n- ${ℓ}^{1},$ the vector space of infinite real sequences where the sum of the absolute values of the terms are finite\n- ${R}_{3000}[t],$ the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $3\\,000$\n- ${\\text{M}}_{50\\times 60}(R),$ the vector space of all $50\\times 60$ matrices over $R$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243985 1072 #uuid "28ab3efd-4668-556c-a241-6c3f64cf9ac7" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243985 1074 "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- ${ℓ}^{1}$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following subset of ${ℓ}^{1}$ is linearly independent: \n\n$$\n{{e}_{1},\\,{e}_{2},\\,…,{e}_{n}},\\,n\\in N\n$$\n\nwhere, for each $j\\in {1,2,…,n},$\n\n$$\n{e}_{j}=(0,…,0,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{1,}_{{↑}_{{j}^{\\text{th}}\\text{ position}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0,…).\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{x}_{i}{e}_{i}=0\n$$\n\nif and only if ${x}_{i}=0$ for all $i=0,1,2,…,n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of ${ℓ}^{1}$ of size $n,$ which imples that  ${ℓ}^{1}$ cannot have finite dimension.\n\n- ${R}_{3000}[t]$ and  ${\\text{M}}_{50\\times 60}(R)$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{3000}[t])=3\\,000+1=3\\,001,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{50\\times 60}(R))=50\\cdot 60=3\\,000\n$$\n\n \nare both finite numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243986 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243533 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243986 1069 "q-106934" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243986 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243986 1071 "In a restaurant kitchen, a pot of soup reaches a temperature of ${200}^{∘}\\text{F}.$ After turning off the stove, the temperature $T(t)$ of the soup decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. If the kitchen temperature is ${76}^{∘}\\text{F},$ which differential equation could be used to model the pot temperature? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243986 1072 #uuid "9d9f1ee5-0c1f-52df-9e3a-c2e55b83891f" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243986 1074 "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={76}^{∘}\\text{F},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-76).\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243987 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243623 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243987 1069 "q-106935" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243987 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243987 1071 "A container of water at a temperature of ${90}^{∘}\\text{C}$ is placed in a room. The temperature $T(t)$ of the water decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is ${19}^{∘}\\text{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the water temperature? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243987 1072 #uuid "4f165134-d2dc-524e-9e90-e4a6ac3b7c02" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243987 1074 "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={19}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-19).\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243988 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243663 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243988 1069 "q-106936" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243988 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243988 1071 "The rate at which a pot of hot oil cools is proportional to the difference between its temperature and the temperature of its surroundings. Let $T(t)$ be the temperature of the liquid in degrees Celsius, and let $t>0$ be the time in minutes. If the temperature of the surroundings is ${30}^{∘}\\text{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the temperature of the oil? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243988 1072 #uuid "431988a0-8981-5d4d-9c5f-dd7ae32d4277" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243988 1074 "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={30}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-30).\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243989 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243774 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243989 1069 "q-106937" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243989 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243989 1071 "A hair straightener reaches a temperature of ${450}^{∘}\\text{F}.$ After turning it off, the temperature $T(t)$ of the straightener decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is ${75}^{∘}\\text{F},$ which differential equation could be used to model the straightener temperature? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243989 1072 #uuid "6cd430a4-4af3-5fcc-b21d-c7663e55325d" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243989 1074 "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={75}^{∘}\\text{F},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-75).\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243990 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243551 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243990 1069 "q-106938" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243990 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243990 1071 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:*\n\n*Given any $x\\in V$, if $y$ is a vector such that $x+y=0$, then $y=-x.$* \n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+y)+(-x) & =0+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (y+x)+(-x) & =(-x)+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A1}\\,] \\\\ (y+x)+(-x) & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ y+(x+(-x)) & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ y+0 & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ y & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243990 1072 #uuid "e67f7f26-7700-547c-83dc-6d0fe900900b" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243990 1074 "Using the vector space axioms, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+y)+(-x) & =0+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (y+x)+(-x) & =(-x)+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A1}\\,] \\\\ (y+x)+(-x) & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A3}\\,\\,] \\\\ y+(x+(-x)) & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A2}\\,\\,] \\\\ y+0 & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ y & =-x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A3}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243991 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243801 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243991 1069 "q-106940" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243991 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243991 1071 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement:*\n\n*If $y$ and $z$ are vectors such that $x+y=z+x$ for some $x\\in V$, then $y=z.$*\n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =z+x &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+y)+(-x) & =(z+x)+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (y+x)+(-x) & =(z+x)+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A1}\\,] \\\\ y+(x+(-x)) & =z+(x+(-x)) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ y+0 & =z+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ y & =z &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243991 1072 #uuid "bf20287f-8e16-5ba4-8bd3-a3b55b06c59c" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243991 1074 "Using the vector space axioms, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =z+x &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (x+y)+(-x) & =(z+x)+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (y+x)+(-x) & =(z+x)+(-x) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A1}\\,] \\\\ y+(x+(-x)) & =z+(x+(-x)) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A2}\\,] \\\\ y+0 & =z+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ y & =z &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A3}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243992 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243807 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243992 1069 "q-106945" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243992 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243992 1071 "An eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ corresponding to an eigenvalue $\\lambda =6$ is  $[\\begin{aligned}c+32 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $c.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243992 1072 #uuid "76913ab7-7467-5c71-9708-e8c16db072c0" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243992 1074 "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}c+32 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] & =6[\\begin{aligned}c+32 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4c+236 \\\\ 2c+28\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6c+192 \\\\ 72\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4c+236=6c+192 \\\\ 2c+28=72\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n2c+28=72\\,⟹\\,c=22.\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n4c+236=6c+192\\,⟹\\,c=22.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $c=22.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243993 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243454 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243993 1069 "q-106946" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243993 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243993 1071 "An eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ is  $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2c\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $c.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243993 1072 #uuid "dcb8db9c-1fc6-56a1-9d1f-daa26c43ddf5" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243993 1074 "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2c\\end{aligned}] & =3[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2c\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1-4c \\\\ -2+8c\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6c\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1-4c=-3 \\\\ -2+8c=6c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n1-4c=-3\\,⟹\\,c=1.\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-2+8c=6c\\,⟹\\,c=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $c=1.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243994 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243994 1069 "q-106958" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243994 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243994 1071 "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$B=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243994 1072 #uuid "8f2440fe-5d7f-50f0-96da-6c8dae104de5" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243994 1074 "First, we find the matrix $\\,B-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nB-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThen, the characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-0\\cdot (-1) & =0 \\\\ 5-5\\lambda -\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+0 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +5 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243995 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243610 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243995 1069 "q-106959" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243995 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243995 1071 "$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 63 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & -1 \\\\ \\boxed{} & \\boxed{} & \\boxed{} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}.\n$$\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. From left to right, what are the missing elements from the second row of the matrix $U?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243995 1072 #uuid "18168df9-7c4c-59e5-8faf-0d6fc1c59cb4" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243995 1074 "The matrix $U$ in the LU decomposition is obtained from the original matrix $A$ by row-reducing using Gaussian elementation. In particular, to make $0$ at the position ${a}_{21}$ of the matrix $A,$ we proceed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 63 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 63 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the elements from the second row of $U$ are $0,3,$ and $-4.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243996 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243860 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243996 1069 "q-106963" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243996 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243996 1071 "$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 7 & 18 & -19\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 9 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\boxed{}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}.\n$$\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. What is the value of ${u}_{33}$ in the matrix $U?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243996 1072 #uuid "0b2eb758-b817-50ae-a571-89f30f2f0315" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243996 1074 "The matrix $U$ in the LU decomposition is obtained from the original matrix $A$ by row-reducing using Gaussian elementation. In particular, to make zeros below the main diagonal, we proceed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 7 & 18 & -19\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 18 & -19\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-7){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 18 & -26\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-9){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${u}_{33}=1.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243997 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243672 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243997 1069 "q-106966" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243997 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243997 1071 "$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 18 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\boxed{} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\n$$\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. What is the value of the entry ${l}_{31}$ in the matrix $L?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243997 1072 #uuid "def7b9dd-c00d-5f8f-b46f-c722adda75d3" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243997 1074 "We want to make $0$ at the position ${a}_{31}$ using Gaussian elimination. To do this, we take the $3$rd row of $A,$ and we add the $1$st row of $A$ multiplied by $-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 18 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 18 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe entry ${l}_{31}$ of the matrix $L$ is the factor next to ${R}_{1},$ taken with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n{l}_{31}=-(-1)=1.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243998 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243500 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243998 1069 "q-106968" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243998 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243998 1071 "$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -12 & -8 & -4 \\\\ 36 & -32 & 46\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 9 & \\boxed{} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\n$$\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. What is the value of the entry ${l}_{32}$ in the matrix $L?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243998 1072 #uuid "656fd206-3946-51f7-a5b6-8c655403f048" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243998 1074 "The matrix $U$ in the LU decomposition is obtained from the original matrix $A$ by row-reducing using Gaussian elementation. In particular, to make zeros below the main diagonal, we proceed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -12 & -8 & -4 \\\\ 36 & -32 & 46\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 5 \\\\ 36 & -32 & 46\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-9){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -32 & 19\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we want to make $0$ at the position ${a}_{32}$ using Gaussian elimination. To do this, we take the $3$rd row of $A,$ and we add the $2$nd row of $A$ multiplied by $-4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -32 & 19\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe entry ${l}_{32}$ of the matrix $L$ is the factor next to ${R}_{2},$ taken with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n{l}_{32}=-(-4)=4.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243999 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243764 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243999 1069 "q-106995" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243999 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243999 1071 "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243999 1072 #uuid "951a8d7c-f3c2-5c26-a5f9-4fbae8462dba" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186243999 1074 "Let's start by writing the matrix $A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-0\\cdot 2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -5)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =5$ and $\\lambda =3.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvalues of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix are the entries on the main diagonal." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244000 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243842 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244000 1069 "q-1070" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244000 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244000 1071 "What is the coefficient matrix for the following system of linear equations?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{1}=3{x}_{3} \\\\ -11{x}_{3}=12\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244000 1072 #uuid "760765ec-b7b6-54ac-b028-0603c846223a" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244000 1074 "First, we organize the system so that the variables are ordered ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}-11{x}_{3}=12\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244001 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243744 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244001 1069 "q-107018" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244001 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244001 1071 "Consider the set ${{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ of polynomials from $R[t],$ where\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=-1+t-2{t}^{2},\\,{p}_{2}(t)=-2,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=t-2{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the set is linearly dependent, find the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}$ from a non-zero solution of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}\\cdot {p}_{2}(t)+{x}_{3}\\cdot {p}_{3}(t)=0.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244001 1072 #uuid "14620d69-e2fb-5946-a678-95eb17b3c5ea" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244001 1074 "If the set is linearly dependent, then there exist ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ not all zero such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)+{x}_{3}{p}_{3}(t)=0.\n$$\n\nOtherwise, if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0,$ the set is linearly independent.\n\nSubstituting the given expressions for ${p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),$ and ${p}_{3}(t)$ into the above equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(-1+t-2{t}^{2})+{x}_{2}(-2)+{x}_{3}(t-2{t}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ (-{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2})+({x}_{1}+{x}_{3})t+(-2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}){t}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation is true for every $t$ if and only if \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding augmented matrix and reduce it to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd. Hence, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. \n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.$ Substitute it into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n-{x}_{1}-2(\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3})=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, for any ${x}_{3}\\ne 0,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}=\\frac{-{x}_{3}}{(\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3})}=-2.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244002 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243865 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244002 1069 "q-107020" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244002 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244002 1071 "Consider the set ${{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ of polynomials from $R[t],$ where\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=-2+2t+2{t}^{2},\\,{p}_{2}(t)=1-4{t}^{2},\\,{p}_{3}(t)=-2+3t-{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIf the set is linearly dependent, find the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{3}}$ from a non-zero solution of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}\\cdot {p}_{2}(t)+{x}_{3}\\cdot {p}_{3}(t)=0.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244002 1072 #uuid "95efe695-ba47-5a50-958d-ff18cf47e088" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244002 1074 "If the set is linearly dependent, then there exist ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ not all zero such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)+{x}_{3}{p}_{3}(t)=0.\n$$\n\nOtherwise, if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0,$ the set is linearly independent.\n\nSubstituting the given expressions for ${p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),$ and ${p}_{3}(t)$ into the above equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(-2+2t+2{t}^{2})+{x}_{2}(1-4{t}^{2})+{x}_{3}(-2+3t-{t}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ (-2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})+(2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{3})t+(2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}){t}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation is true for every $t$ if and only if \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding augmented matrix and reduce it to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd. Hence, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable.\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{