bf-a816-93e7357a6fc5" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244096 1074 "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{5} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =2 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2) \\\\  & =1.107\\,\\text{rad}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & =(\\sqrt{5})^{2}+(2\\sqrt{5})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =5+4\\cdot 5 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =25 \\\\ R & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)≈5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+1.107)\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244097 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243447 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244097 1069 "q-107578" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244097 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244097 1071 "Express $z=3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}))$ in the form $z=x+\\text{i}y.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244097 1072 #uuid "771ab1fb-0873-549b-b5d0-5795adc724e8" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244097 1074 "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=0$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =3(0-\\text{i}) \\\\  & =-3\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244098 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243675 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244098 1069 "q-107579" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244098 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244098 1071 "Given that the expression $2\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ)$ with $0<ϕ<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244098 1072 #uuid "b5b0b758-0715-5d36-8c00-3c3db2730695" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244098 1074 "The difference formula for cosine gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\ ϕ & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244099 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243652 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244099 1069 "q-107587" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244099 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244099 1071 "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n- ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n- ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+{c}_{3}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244099 1072 #uuid "6d745992-cf32-533c-af78-280d0db594aa" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244099 1074 "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{2},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the set contains a vector from another vector space.\n\n- Statement II is false. The set ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{2}$ because the set contains vectors from another vector space.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}+{x}_{3}({c}_{1}+{c}_{3}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1}+{x}_{3}){c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}+{x}_{3}{c}_{3} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+{c}_{3}}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $C$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+{c}_{3}}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}+{x}_{3}{c}_{3}=({x}_{1}-{x}_{3}){c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}+{x}_{3}({c}_{1}+{c}_{3})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244100 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243864 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244100 1069 "q-107588" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244100 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244100 1071 "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3},x}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$\n- ${{b}_{1},2{b}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n- ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},2{c}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244100 1072 #uuid "013a3d9d-c3ab-5357-a425-3057635e56b4" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244100 1074 "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{2},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$, any vector of ${R}^{3},$ including the given vector $x,$ can be written as a linear combination of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}.$ This means that ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3},x}$ is linearly dependent and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}({b}_{1})+{x}_{2}(2{b}_{2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+(2{x}_{2}){b}_{2} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, ${{b}_{1},2{b}_{2}}$ spans ${R}^{2}$ since any linear combination of $B$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${{b}_{1},2{b}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}={x}_{1}{b}_{1}+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{2}(2{b}_{2})\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. The set ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},2{c}_{2}}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the third vector is a linear combination of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ This means that the set is linearly dependent and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244101 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243576 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244101 1069 "q-107589" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244101 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244101 1071 "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ be bases of ${R}^{3}$ and ${R}^{2}$, respectively. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},x}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n- ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$\n- ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244101 1072 #uuid "fba7ca1e-918b-5610-87e4-9553b3a87d7f" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244101 1074 "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{2},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$, any vector of ${R}^{2},$ including the given vector $x,$ can be written as a linear combination of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ This means that ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},x}$ is linearly dependent and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\n- Statement II is false. The set ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the vector ${b}_{3}$ is in ${R}^{3}$ but it is not a linear combination of  ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ as the set  ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ is linearly independent. This means that the set does not span ${R}^{3}$ and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\n- Statement III is false. The set ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the set contains vectors from another vector space.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true\"." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244102 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243773 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244102 1069 "q-107654" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244102 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244102 1071 "Express $S=\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{5}{4}+\\frac{5}{6}+⋯+\\frac{5}{26}+\\frac{5}{28}$ in sigma notation." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244102 1072 #uuid "d6ba56d2-f854-5dee-b439-93d19421dc18" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244102 1074 "We first examine the numerators and denominators of the corresponding sequence.\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2},\\,\\frac{5}{4},\\,\\frac{5}{6},\\,…,\\,\\frac{5}{26},\\,\\frac{5}{28}\n$$\n\nOur goal is to find a pattern in the terms. To do this, we note the following:\n\n- The numerators form the following sequence:\n\n$$\n5,\\,5,\\,5,\\,…,\\,5,\\,5\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of this sequence is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=5.\n$$\n\n- The denominators form the following sequence:\n\n$$\n2,\\,4,\\,…,\\,26,\\,28\n$$\n\nThese values are multiples of $2,$ and since $28÷2=14,$ there are $14$ terms in total. The $n$th term of this sequence is given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=2n,\\,1\\le n\\le 14.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $n$th term of the sequence\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2},\\,\\frac{5}{4},\\,\\frac{5}{6},\\,…,\\,\\frac{5}{26},\\,\\frac{5}{28}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{b}_{n}}=\\frac{5}{2n},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ ranges from $n=1$ to $n=14.$ \n\nTherefore, we can express our series using sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S={\\sum }_{n=1}^{14}\\frac{5}{2n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244103 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243650 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244103 1069 "q-107669" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244103 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244103 1071 "The cubic polynomial $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=3,{x}_{2,3}=\\pm 5\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $2.$ Find $f(x).$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244103 1072 #uuid "3c8eacb5-c4fc-55c6-a117-32cf498dd072" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244103 1074 "Since $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=3$ and ${x}_{2,3}=\\pm 2\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=2,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =2(x-3)(x-5\\text{i})(x-(-5\\text{i})) \\\\  & =2(x-3)(x-5\\text{i})(x+5\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x-3)(x-5\\text{i})(x+5\\text{i}) & = \\\\ 2(x-3)({x}^{2}-(5\\text{i})^{2}) & = \\\\ 2(x-3)({x}^{2}+25) & = \\\\ 2({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+25x-75) & = \\\\ 2{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+50x-150 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+50x-150.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244104 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243884 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244104 1069 "q-107670" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244104 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244104 1071 "![](images/a9/a926400e24bc00ca9027d44461e800fa22015ff8289f05fba3a8b51f8b72cce5.png)\n\nThe Argand diagram above shows the roots of the cubic polynomial $f(x).$ Find $f(x)$ given that the coefficient of the cubic term is $4.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244104 1072 #uuid "2decd930-e39a-52cd-b5e4-e2dcc753b106" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244104 1074 "From the diagram, we see that $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=0$ and ${x}_{2,3}=2\\pm 2\\text{i}.$ Additionally, we're given that the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=4.$ Therefore, we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =4(x-0)(x-(2+2\\text{i}))(x-(2-2\\text{i})) \\\\  & =4x(x-(2+2\\text{i}))(x-(2-2\\text{i}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x(x-(2+2\\text{i}))(x-(2-2\\text{i})) & = \\\\ 4x({x}^{2}-x(2-2\\text{i})-x(2+2\\text{i})+(2+2\\text{i})(2-2\\text{i})) & = \\\\ 4x({x}^{2}-4x+8) & = \\\\ 4{x}^{3}-16{x}^{2}+32x & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=4{x}^{3}-16{x}^{2}+32x.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244105 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243448 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244105 1069 "q-107681" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244105 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244105 1071 "The cubic polynomial $P(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=-1,$ ${x}_{2}=4,$ and ${x}_{3}=5,$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $1.$ Find $P(x).$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244105 1072 #uuid "ca6d9f00-d56e-512c-bce4-501d6e27c705" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244105 1074 "Since our cubic polynomial has roots ${x}_{1}=-1,$ ${x}_{1}=4,$ and ${x}_{3}=5,$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=1,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3}) & =1(x-(-1))(x-4)(x-5) \\\\  & =(x+1)(x-4)(x-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)(x-4)(x-5) & =({x}^{2}-3x-4)(x-5) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-5{x}^{2}+15x-4x+20 \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+11x+20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)={x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+11x+20.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244106 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243693 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244106 1069 "q-10769" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244106 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244106 1071 "Find the radius of convergence $R$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{n}^{2}(x+1)^{n}=(x+1)+{2}^{2}(x+1)^{2}+{3}^{2}(x+1)^{3}+⋯\\,.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244106 1072 #uuid "ad3752e9-ac56-5e6a-a8ab-a41a282342a4" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244106 1074 "To apply the ratio test. we define ${a}_{n}={n}^{2}(x+1)^{n}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(n+1)^{2}(x+1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}(x+1)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(n+1)^{2}(x+1)}{{n}^{2}}| \\\\  & =|x+1|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{n+1}{n})}^{2} \\\\  & =|x+1|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|x+1|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if $|x+1|<1.$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=1.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244107 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243766 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244107 1069 "q-107695" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244107 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244107 1071 "The quadratic polynomial $P(y)$ has the double root ${y}_{1}={y}_{2}=4,$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=3.$ Find $P(y).$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244107 1072 #uuid "e58cf3f2-d831-56b3-ae64-968d9ec9277c" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244107 1074 "Since our quadratic polynomial has the double root ${y}_{1}={y}_{2}=4,$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=3,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(y-{y}_{1})^{2} & =3{(y-4)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{(y-4)}^{2} & =3({y}^{2}-8y+16) \\\\  & =3{y}^{2}-24y+48.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(y)=3{y}^{2}-24y+48.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244108 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243619 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244108 1069 "q-107696" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244108 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244108 1071 "Which of the following is a factor of ${y}^{2}-8y+17?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244108 1072 #uuid "963cb1b5-4672-5772-af3f-0514a1529c6d" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244108 1074 "First, we let $f(y)={y}^{2}-8y+17.$\n\nNext, we solve $f(y)=0$ by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-8y+17 & =0 \\\\ ({y}^{2}-8y+{4}^{2})-{4}^{2}+17 & =0 \\\\ (y-4)^{2}-16+17 & =0 \\\\ (y-4)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & ={\\text{i}}^{2} \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =(\\text{i})^{2} \\\\ y-4 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ y & =4\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our quadratic equation has roots ${y}_{1}=4+\\text{i}$ and ${y}_{2}=4-\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=1,$ the expression ${y}^{2}-8y+17$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-8y+17 & =a(y-{y}_{1})(y-{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =(y-(4+\\text{i}))(y-(4-\\text{i})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, among the given options, only $y-(4+\\text{i})$ is a factor of ${y}^{2}-8y+17.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244109 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243723 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244109 1069 "q-107697" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244109 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244109 1071 "Which of the following is a factor of $3{z}^{2}-24z+75?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244109 1072 #uuid "08fb3447-ef6d-5299-8ee8-8365ce0ff2df" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244109 1074 "First, we let $f(z)=3{z}^{2}-24z+75.$\n\nNext, we solve $f(z)=0$ by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(z) & =0 \\\\ 3{z}^{2}-24z+75 & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2}-8z+25 & =0 \\\\ ({z}^{2}-2\\cdot 4\\cdot z+{4}^{2})-{4}^{2}+25 & =0 \\\\ (z-4)^{2}-16+25 & =0 \\\\ (z-4)^{2}+9 & =0 \\\\ (z-4)^{2} & =-9 \\\\ (z-4)^{2} & =9{\\text{i}}^{2} \\\\ (z-4)^{2} & =(3\\text{i})^{2} \\\\ z-4 & =\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ z & =4\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our quadratic equation has roots ${z}_{1}=4+3\\text{i}$ and ${z}_{2}=4-3\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=3,$ the expression $3{z}^{2}-24z+75$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}^{2}-24z+75 & =a(z-{z}_{1})(z-{z}_{2}) \\\\  & =3(z-(4+3\\text{i}))(z-(4-3\\text{i})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, among the given options, only $z-(4-3\\text{i})$ is a factor of $3{z}^{2}-24z+75.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244110 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243519 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244110 1069 "q-107795" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244110 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244110 1071 "The quartic polynomial $P(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=1,{x}_{2}=2,{x}_{3}=-4$ and ${x}_{4}=4$ and the coefficient of the quartic term is $1.$ Find $P(x).$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244110 1072 #uuid "1f1849e7-d472-55dd-8262-ff8adf785460" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244110 1074 "Since our quartic polynomial has roots ${x}_{1}=1,{x}_{2}=2,$ ${x}_{3}=-4,$ and ${x}_{4}=4,$ and the coefficient of the quartic term is $a=1,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3})(x-{x}_{4}) & =1(x-1)(x-2)(x-(-4))(x-4) \\\\  & =(x-1)(x-2)(x+4)(x-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)(x-2)(x+4)(x-4) & =({x}^{2}-3x+2)({x}^{2}-16) \\\\  & ={x}^{4}-16{x}^{2}-3{x}^{3}+48x+2{x}^{2}-32 \\\\  & ={x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}-14{x}^{2}+48x-32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)={x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}-14{x}^{2}+48x-32.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244111 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243819 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244111 1069 "q-107815" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244111 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244111 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x+y}{3-2x-y}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|g(x,y)|=-\\frac{x+y}{3}+C\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244111 1072 #uuid "d0db4ae1-7ca8-5d44-90b5-79a2a42ad1ee" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244111 1074 "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x+y}{3-(2x+y)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=2x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2+\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2 & =\\frac{z}{3-z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{6-z}{3-z}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{3-z}{6-z})\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int (\\frac{z-3}{z-6})\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (\\frac{3}{z-6}+1)\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|z-6|+z & =x+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+y-6|+2x+y & =x+K \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+y-6| & =-\\frac{x+y}{3}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=\\frac{K}{3}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=2x+y-6.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244112 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243814 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244112 1069 "q-107819" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244112 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244112 1071 "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 10\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244112 1072 #uuid "0282c952-693d-5dc9-bc8b-6f8644d776fa" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244112 1074 "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=4v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =4.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244113 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243537 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244113 1069 "q-107837" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244113 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244113 1071 "An eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}11 & -6 \\\\ -12 & 25\\end{aligned}]$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7$ is  $v=[\\begin{aligned}c+1 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $c.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244113 1072 #uuid "e548fde0-64fe-56ce-a880-74d36f5eace9" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244113 1074 "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}11 & -6 \\\\ -12 & 25\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}c+1 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}] & =7[\\begin{aligned}c+1 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5c+11 \\\\ 13c-12\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}7c+7 \\\\ 7c\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5c+11=7c+7 \\\\ 13c-12=7c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n5c+11=7c+7\\,⟹\\,c=2.\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n13c-12=7c\\,⟹\\,c=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $c=2.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244114 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243763 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244114 1069 "q-107842" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244114 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244114 1071 "The eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -8+k \\\\ 3 & 5k\\end{aligned}]$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2k$ is  $[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $k.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244114 1072 #uuid "242d3574-f624-5b15-a035-897cf33da567" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244114 1074 "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & -8+k \\\\ 3 & 5k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =2k[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}k-10 \\\\ -6+5k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-4k \\\\ 2k\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}k-10=-4k, \\\\ -6+5k=2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $k$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nk-10=-4k\\,⟹\\,k=2.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $k=2$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6+5k & =2k \\\\ -6+5(2) & =2(2) \\\\ 4 & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $k=2.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244115 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243460 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244115 1069 "q-107843" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244115 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244115 1071 "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$N=[\\begin{aligned}18 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244115 1072 #uuid "f128d753-12d7-51df-b895-9c1460388388" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244115 1074 "Let's start by writing the matrix $N-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nN-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}18-\\lambda  & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(N-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(N-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}18-\\lambda  & -5 \\\\ 0 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (18-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-0\\cdot (-5) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -18) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda =18.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvalues of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix are the entries on the main diagonal." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244116 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243564 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244116 1069 "q-107852" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244116 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244116 1071 "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$Q=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244116 1072 #uuid "41123383-6ac1-5b34-8d5e-987d1637c699" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244116 1074 "Let's start by writing the matrix $Q-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nQ-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -3 & 8-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(Q-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Q-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -3 & 8-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(8-\\lambda )-(-3)\\cdot 1 & =0 \\\\ 40-5\\lambda -8\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+3 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +43 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the discriminant of the quadratic equation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(-13)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 43 \\\\  & =169-172 \\\\  & =-3 \\\\  & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the characteristic equation does not have real solutions and therefore $Q$ does not have real eigenvalues." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244117 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243735 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244117 1069 "q-107987" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244117 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244117 1071 "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$B=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -5\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244117 1072 #uuid "11ba9d38-2921-5184-bf8a-8d9ae2f12bf8" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244117 1074 "First, we find the matrix $\\,B-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nB-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}8-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 6 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThen, the characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}8-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 6 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (8-\\lambda )(-5-\\lambda )-6\\cdot 0 & =0 \\\\ -40-8\\lambda +5\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -40 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244118 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243575 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244118 1069 "q-1080" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244118 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244118 1071 "Calculate $\\text{det}(X)$, if\n    $X=[\\begin{aligned}10 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244118 1072 #uuid "63888dba-48b5-5525-9ea0-6080e9970645" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244118 1074 "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}|=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(X) & =|\\begin{aligned}10 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =10\\cdot 4-(-3)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =40-(-3) \\\\  & =43\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244119 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243792 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244119 1069 "q-108008" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244119 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244119 1071 "Given that $\\lambda =-5$ and $\\lambda =6$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244119 1072 #uuid "d85fab59-8e48-543a-b7a0-bd15b756ce1e" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244119 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =-5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]-(-5)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}9{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{2}{9}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-9{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -9 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{9}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244120 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243802 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244120 1069 "q-108039" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244120 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244120 1071 "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244120 1072 #uuid "e9180a9d-8bb8-5d9a-bdf7-f88ca64062d6" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244120 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =7.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-7[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-3{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{1}{3}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244121 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243858 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244121 1069 "q-108044" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244121 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244121 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1.$\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3.$\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2,$ then $3v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-6.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244121 1072 #uuid "5fa81e71-e4f5-5405-bfdd-41cd7387b276" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244121 1074 "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $Av=v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =1$ (but not $-1$).\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-3v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $Av=-3v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(3v)=3Av=3(-2v)=-2(3v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(3v)=-2(3v),$ and $3v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $3v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-2$ (but not $-6$).\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244122 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243643 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244122 1069 "q-108275" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244122 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244122 1071 "Consider the equation ${x}^{4}+6{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}+18x+27=0.$ Given that $x=-3$ is a solution of multiplicity $2,$ find the remaining solutions." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244122 1072 #uuid "456491e9-c998-5954-8ed1-c8e62702afad" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244122 1074 "Let $f(x)={x}^{4}+6{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}+18x+27.$ Since ${x}_{1,2}=-3$ is a root of multiplicity $2,$ according to the factor theorem, \n\n$$\n(x+3)^{2}={x}^{2}+6x+9\n$$\n\nis a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nNow, we can use long division to find the quotient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}+3 \\\\  & {x}^{2}+6x+9\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}+6{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}+18x+27\\,\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{4}+6{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,3{x}^{2}+18x+27 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{3{x}^{2}+18x+27}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\nf(x)=({x}^{2}+6x+9)({x}^{2}+3).\n$$\n\nTo find the other two roots, we solve ${x}^{2}+3=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-3 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i})^{2} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots of our quartic equation are $x=-3,\\,-3,\\,\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244123 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243465 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244123 1069 "q-1083" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244123 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244123 1071 "$|\\begin{aligned}2x & {y}^{2} \\\\ z & -3\\end{aligned}|=$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244123 1072 #uuid "04f24cee-9b4e-520b-8022-59737dad510a" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244123 1074 "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}|=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2x & {y}^{2} \\\\ z & -3\\end{aligned}| & =2x\\cdot (-3)-{y}^{2}\\cdot z \\\\  & =-6x-{y}^{2}z\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244124 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243540 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244124 1069 "q-10830" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244124 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244124 1071 "Find where the tangent to the curve $y=4\\sqrt{x}-4x$ at  $(1,0)$ intersects the $y$-axis." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244124 1072 #uuid "830a020d-d73a-537f-8613-ed95fff36b75" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244124 1074 "Let $f(x)=4\\sqrt{x}-4x.$ Let's first calculate the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\sqrt{x}-4x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{1/2}-4x) \\\\  & =2{x}^{-1/2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at our point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(1)=2(1)^{-1/2}-4=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(0) & =-2(x-1) \\\\ y & =-2x+2-0 \\\\ y & =-2x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the tangent line intercepts the $y$-axis, we set $x=0$ and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2(0)+2=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intersection point is $(0,2).$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244125 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243876 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244125 1069 "q-108311" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244125 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244125 1071 "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:r=⟨5,-2,3⟩+t⟨0,3,-1⟩,\\,Π:r\\cdot ⟨1,3,-2⟩=-7.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244125 1072 #uuid "96c2dbc6-8bc6-5fb7-8e78-32cd42df2601" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244125 1074 "Let's write the equation of the line as\n\n$$\nr=⟨5,-2+3t,3-t⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $r=⟨5,-2+3t,3-t⟩$ into the equation of the plane, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot ⟨1,3,-2⟩ & =-7 \\\\ ⟨5,-2+3t,3-t⟩\\cdot ⟨1,3,-2⟩ & =-7 \\\\ 5+3(-2+3t)+(-2)(3-t) & =-7 \\\\ 5-6+9t-6+2t & =-7 \\\\ 11t & =0 \\\\ t & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, position vector $p$ of the intersection point $P$ is obtained by substituting $t=0$ into the equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =⟨5,-2+3(0),3-0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,-2,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intersection point is $P(5,-2,3).$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244126 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243800 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244126 1069 "q-108313" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244126 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244126 1071 "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:r=⟨3,0,-1⟩+t⟨5,-2,4⟩,\\,Π:r\\cdot ⟨0,1,-2⟩=22.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244126 1072 #uuid "14540267-d662-5ba9-9bd7-2ba100d932e1" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244126 1074 "Let's write the equation of the line as\n\n$$\nr=⟨3+5t,-2t,-1+4t⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $r=⟨3+5t,-2t,-1+4t⟩$ into the equation of the plane, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot ⟨0,1,-2⟩ & =22 \\\\ ⟨3+5t,-2t,-1+4t⟩\\cdot ⟨0,1,-2⟩ & =22 \\\\ 0(3+5t)+1(-2t)+(-2)(-1+4t) & =22 \\\\ -2t+2-8t & =22 \\\\ -10t & =20 \\\\ t & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, position vector $p$ of the intersection point $P$ is obtained by substituting $t=-2$ into the equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =⟨3+5(-2),-2(-2),-1+4(-2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-7,4,-9⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intersection point is $P(-7,4,-9).$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244127 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243645 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244127 1069 "q-108323" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244127 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244127 1071 "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:{\\begin{aligned}x=7+2t \\\\ y=3+t \\\\ z=1-t\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,Π:x-3y+2z=12.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244127 1072 #uuid "7d4770f9-017d-5451-ade2-ef9bc61cba33" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244127 1074 "We can substitute the parametric expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ of the line into the equation of the plane immediately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3y+2z & =12 \\\\ 1(7+2t)-3(3+t)+2(1-t) & =12 \\\\ 7+2t-9-3t+2-2t & =12 \\\\ -3t & =12 \\\\ t & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get the position vector $p$ of the intersection point $P$, we substitute $t=-4$ into the parametric equations of the line:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=7+2(-4)=-1 \\\\ y=3+(-4)=-1 \\\\ z=1-(-4)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection point is $P(-1,-1,5).$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244128 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243844 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244128 1069 "q-108325" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244128 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244128 1071 "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:{\\begin{aligned}x=2-3t \\\\ y=-3+t \\\\ z=4+2t\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,Π:3x+y-4z+45=0.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244128 1072 #uuid "23026cc4-61f1-51ff-9518-0d934ee1dfc2" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244128 1074 "We can substitute the parametric expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ of the line into the equation of the plane immediately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+y-4z+45 & =0 \\\\ 3(2-3t)+(-3+t)-4(4+2t) & =-45 \\\\ 6-9t-3+t-16-8t & =-45 \\\\ -16t & =-32 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get the position vector $p$ of the intersection point $P$, we substitute $t=2$ into the parametric equations of the line:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2-3(2)=-4 \\\\ y=-3+2=-1 \\\\ z=4+2(2)=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection point is  $P(-4,-1,8).$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244129 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243468 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244129 1069 "q-10833" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244129 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244129 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=3{x}^{2}+4x$ at the point $(1,7).$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244129 1072 #uuid "bf96ee12-3f6e-5eba-a49b-e0ee94663597" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244129 1074 "Let $f(x)=3{x}^{2}+4x.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}+4x)=6x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(1)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1)=6(1)+4=10\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=10$ and the coordinates of our point $(1,7),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(7) & =10(x-1) \\\\ y & =10x-10+7 \\\\ y & =10x-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244130 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243778 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244130 1069 "q-108337" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244130 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244130 1071 "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:{\\begin{aligned}x=9-3t \\\\ y=5+2t \\\\ z=-7+t\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,Π:4x+3y+5z-11=0.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244130 1072 #uuid "ba20f45e-ea9a-5cbd-a036-8965c362108b" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244130 1074 "We can substitute the parametric expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ of the line into the equation of the plane immediately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+3y+5z-11 & =0 \\\\ 4(9-3t)+3(5+2t)+5(-7+t) & =11 \\\\ 36-12t+15+6t-35+5t & =11 \\\\ -t & =-5 \\\\ t & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get the position vector $p$ of the intersection point $P$, we substitute $t=5$ into the parametric equations of the line:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=9-3(5)=-6 \\\\ y=5+2(5)=15 \\\\ z=-7+5=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection point is  $P(-6,15,-2).$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244131 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243648 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244131 1069 "q-108344" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244131 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244131 1071 "Find all of the solutions to ${x}^{4}-6{x}^{3}+10{x}^{2}-30x+25=0$ given that $x=\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i}$ is a solution." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244131 1072 #uuid "f3228df1-b9d4-5745-9fec-ae28dacb4d65" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244131 1074 "First, we let $f(x)={x}^{4}-6{x}^{3}+10{x}^{2}-30x+25.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i}}=-\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$(x-\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i})(x-(-\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i}))$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i})(x-(-\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i})) & =(x-\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i})(x+\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-(\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i})^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}+5$ using long division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}-6x+5\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & {x}^{2}+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3}+10{x}^{2}-30x+25\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-6{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}-30x \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-6{x}^{3}-30x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,5{x}^{2}+25 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{5{x}^{2}+25}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}+5)({x}^{2}-6x+5).$\n\nTo find the other two roots, we solve ${x}^{2}-6x+5=0:$\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-6x+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-5) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Therefore, the roots of our quartic equation are $x=\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i},\\,1,\\,5.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244132 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243757 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244132 1069 "q-10835" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244132 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244132 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve  $y=2{x}^{2}+8x$ at the point $(2,24).$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244132 1072 #uuid "d0e5b8a4-9dc2-5fdd-9f7d-f9d1860644a8" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244132 1074 "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+8x.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2}+8x)=4x+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(2)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(2)=4(2)+8=16\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=16$ and the coordinates of our point $(2,24),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(24) & =16(x-2) \\\\ y & =16x-32+24 \\\\ y & =16x-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244133 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243618 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244133 1069 "q-108367" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244133 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244133 1071 "Given that ${y}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)-2{x}^{2}=5,$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244133 1072 #uuid "673feb27-4ae3-559c-903e-378e7e84b56d" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244133 1074 "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)-2{x}^{2} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)-2{x}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y))-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})-4x & =0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(3{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)) & =4x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4x}{3{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244134 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243868 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244134 1069 "q-10837" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244134 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244134 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=5{x}^{2}+4x$ at the point $(1,9).$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244134 1072 #uuid "8e99979c-2ce7-5d29-8201-3e59e8d5c01e" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244134 1074 "Let $f(x)=5{x}^{2}+4x.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{2}+4x)=10x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(1)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1)=10(1)+4=14\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=14$ and the coordinates of our point $(1,9),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(9) & =14(x-1) \\\\ y & =14x-14+9 \\\\ y & =14x-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244135 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243470 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244135 1069 "q-10839" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244135 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244135 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y={x}^{2}-3x$ at the point $(-2,10).$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244135 1072 #uuid "ac2b6c08-7044-5ff0-8a22-7d7df849af22" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244135 1074 "Let $f(x)={x}^{2}-3x.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-3x)=2x-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(-2)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2)=2(-2)-3=-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=-7$ and the coordinates of our point $(-2,10),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(10) & =-7(x-(-2)) \\\\ y-10 & =-7(x+2) \\\\ y & =-7x-14+10 \\\\ y & =-7x-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244136 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243768 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244136 1069 "q-10841" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244136 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244136 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y={x}^{2}-6x$ at the point $(3,-9).$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244136 1072 #uuid "59f7c071-253d-534f-b5b0-303aefe3edb7" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244136 1074 "Let $f(x)={x}^{2}-6x.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-6x)=2x-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(3)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(3)=2(3)-6=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=0$ and the coordinates of our point $(3,-9),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-9) & =0(x-3) \\\\ y & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244137 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243542 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244137 1069 "q-10844" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244137 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244137 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=2{x}^{2}-5x$ at the point $(3,3).$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244137 1072 #uuid "efd8826d-84db-5bb6-981e-e0d1d3969158" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244137 1074 "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{2}-5x.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2}-5x)=4x-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(3)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(3)=4(3)-5=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=7$ and the coordinates of our point $(3,3),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(3) & =7(x-3) \\\\ y & =7x-21+3 \\\\ y & =7x-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244138 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243706 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244138 1069 "q-108448" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244138 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244138 1071 "If the speed of a rabbit is measured in $\\text{in/min},$ what are the units of its acceleration?" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244138 1072 #uuid "74f37dee-395d-5c55-b0ce-e5d3a65d3713" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244138 1074 "We can find the acceleration of an object by computing the slope of its speed-time graph. So, let's draw an example of a speed-time graph where the speed is measured in $\\text{in/min}$ and the time is measured in $\\text{min}.$\n\n![](images/ec/ecdd2f132499a6be0c1d8fa48f021713e2019ed9e6660af041853f7e30766091.png)\n\nIn this case, the slope is computed as\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{\\text{change in speed}}{\\text{change in time}},\n$$\n\nand so the units of the slope are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{units of speed}}{\\text{units of time}}=\\frac{\\text{in/min}}{\\text{min}}=\\frac{\\text{in}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\text{min}}=\\frac{\\text{in}}{{\\text{min}}^{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the units of acceleration are $\\frac{\\text{in}}{{\\text{min}}^{2}}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244139 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243834 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244139 1069 "q-10845" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244139 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244139 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}+7x$ at the point $(1,9).$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244139 1072 #uuid "cac0735c-f86d-5b6c-8133-f4de7a4b7611" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244139 1074 "Let $f(x)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}+7x.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}+7x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{-5}+7x) \\\\  & =-10{x}^{-6}+7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(1)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1)=-10(1{)}^{-6}+7=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=-3$ and the coordinates of our point $(1,9),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(9) & =-3(x-1) \\\\ y & =-3x+3+9 \\\\ y & =-3x+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244140 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243638 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244140 1069 "q-108451" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244140 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244140 1071 "If the speed of a turtle is measured in $\\text{cm/min},$ what are the units of its acceleration?" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244140 1072 #uuid "934b2236-ab64-5d45-9b31-7367f74fc2fc" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244140 1074 "We can find the acceleration of an object by computing the slope of its speed-time graph. So, let's draw an example of a speed-time graph where the speed is measured in $\\text{cm/min}$ and the time is measured in $\\text{min}.$\n\n![](images/e5/e525458fbae05a5ec2f7cb79240d464c7d216a977d489eff473f17c8edaa300b.png)\n\nIn this case, the slope is computed as\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{\\text{change in speed}}{\\text{change in time}},\n$$\n\nand so the units of the slope are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{units of speed}}{\\text{units of time}}=\\frac{\\text{cm/min}}{\\text{min}}=\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\text{min}}=\\frac{\\text{cm}}{{\\text{min}}^{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the units of acceleration are $\\frac{\\text{cm}}{{\\text{min}}^{2}}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244141 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243859 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244141 1069 "q-108454" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244141 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244141 1071 "If the speed of an airplane is measured in $\\text{mi/hour},$ what are the units of its acceleration?" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244141 1072 #uuid "d5ae2998-60ef-527c-8ff4-3d1805a98225" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244141 1074 "We can find the acceleration of an object by computing the slope of its speed-time graph. So, let's draw an example of a speed-time graph where the speed is measured in $\\text{mi/hour}$ and the time is measured in $\\text{hours}.$\n\n![](images/10/10e3d32b8d1fc512f6ced10dacc5b1c3df2dc1e7564c739a459b8661c63c68c6.png)\n\nIn this case, the slope is computed as\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{\\text{change in speed}}{\\text{change in time}},\n$$\n\nand so the units of the slope are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{units of speed}}{\\text{units of time}}=\\frac{\\text{mi/hour}}{\\text{hour}}=\\frac{\\text{mi}}{\\text{hour}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\text{hour}}=\\frac{\\text{mi}}{{\\text{hour}}^{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the units of acceleration are $\\frac{\\text{mi}}{{\\text{hour}}^{2}}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244142 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243711 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244142 1069 "q-108456" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244142 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244142 1071 "![](images/35/352f3d049bd57a805419a34a7873f37f30c95cb2c7c28f4cac984584ff646c75.png)\n\nThe speed-time graph represents the motion of a bus. Calculate the acceleration of the bus." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244142 1072 #uuid "9a1def4d-897d-59c7-aa69-f1b5d63c4555" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244142 1074 "In a speed-time graph, the acceleration is given by the slope of the line.\n\nThe line passes through the points $(0,0)$ and $(4,2),$ so its slope is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{2\\,\\text{m/s}-0\\,\\text{m/s}}{4\\,\\text{s}-0\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\,\\text{m/s}}{4\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration of the bus is $0.5\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244143 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243775 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244143 1069 "q-108458" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244143 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244143 1071 "![](images/0c/0c1aa4aba4a5d8ea2a00bbb5e94bcc61764ed97a6bf8b434138310d45f7cd35d.png)\n\nThe speed-time graph represents the motion of a $250\\text{cc}$ motorcycle at the start of a race. Calculate the acceleration of the motorcycle." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244143 1072 #uuid "33f2a533-8b11-5f60-aa9c-11e8b7d80410" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244143 1074 "In a speed-time graph, the acceleration is given by the slope of the line.\n\nThe line passes through the points $(0,0)$ and $(8,28),$ so its slope is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{28\\,\\text{m/s}-0\\,\\text{m/s}}{8\\,\\text{s}-0\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =\\frac{28\\,\\text{m/s}}{8\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =3.5\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration of the $250\\text{cc}$ motorcycle is $3.5\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244144 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243546 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244144 1069 "q-108464" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244144 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244144 1071 "![](images/f7/f74fdd298fb73e3bd7e3587c18c1f6ffc8fa6062d52daa07c7158b341c6e8879.png)\n\nThe speed-time graph shown above represents the motion of a light aircraft. At what times is the aircraft decelerating?" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244144 1072 #uuid "2c9e189f-eec1-57f8-998c-5e52fe8c0ad4" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244144 1074 "The light aircraft is decelerating when its acceleration is negative. In a speed-time graph, the acceleration is given by the slope of the line. So, we need to find the times for which the slope is negative.\n\nWe see from the graph that the slope is negative from $t=0\\,\\text{min}$ to $t=3\\,\\text{min}.$ Therefore, the light aircraft is decelerating in this time interval." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244145 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243689 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244145 1069 "q-108468" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244145 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244145 1071 "![](images/94/94807e919e7e2cb3f281e53de88334bd6d50dadb1de93beb2a8adc04148c5756.png)\n\nThe speed-time graph shown above represents the motion of an ostrich. At what times is the ostrich decelerating?" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244145 1072 #uuid "7adea4da-2e2a-53f2-be67-cb0c1048dec0" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244145 1074 "The ostrich is decelerating when its acceleration is negative. In a speed-time graph, the acceleration is given by the slope of the line. So, we need to find the times for which the slope is negative.\n\nWe see from the graph that the slope is negative from $t=5\\,\\text{s}$ to $t=8\\,\\text{s}.$ Therefore, the ostrich is decelerating in this time interval." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244146 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243570 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244146 1069 "q-108475" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244146 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244146 1071 "Given that $\\operatorname{log}⁡(y)+3{x}^{2}=11,$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244146 1072 #uuid "ee119daa-efcf-505f-b183-08b031d8df4a" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244146 1074 "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(y)+3{x}^{2} & =11 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{log}⁡(y)+3{x}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(11) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{log}⁡(y))+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+6x & =0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-6x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(-6x)(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)\\,xy\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244147 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243616 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244147 1069 "q-108506" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244147 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244147 1071 "Given that  ${e}^{y}=2y-x,$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244147 1072 #uuid "7ce22a4d-4733-52a4-87c2-c2e0529b4bfc" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244147 1074 "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2y-x) \\\\ {e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}(2y)}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{\\text{d}(x)}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ {e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(2-{e}^{y}) & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2-{e}^{y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244148 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243869 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244148 1069 "q-108509" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244148 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244148 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for ${x}^{3}{e}^{y}=9{x}^{4}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244148 1072 #uuid "7f4c216f-b191-5d84-8924-850e05c75712" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244148 1074 "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{3}{e}^{y}$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}{e}^{y} & =9{x}^{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}{e}^{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(9{x}^{4}) \\\\ [({x}^{3})({e}^{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+(3{x}^{2})({e}^{y})] & =36{x}^{3} \\\\ {x}^{3}\\,{e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}{e}^{y} & =36{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}{e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}{e}^{y} & =36{x}^{3} \\\\ {x}^{3}{e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =36{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}{e}^{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{36{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}{e}^{y}}{{x}^{3}{e}^{y}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{36}{{e}^{y}}-\\frac{3}{x}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244149 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243796 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244149 1069 "q-108581" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244149 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244149 1071 "![](images/89/89b0be0ca7ae1a6e76d32bfc669d6f554deaaad2055b2875e3f6a49821043205.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244149 1072 #uuid "dda73782-2027-59b5-9ca3-37b046a17959" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244149 1074 "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n![](images/cf/cfb095836b7498c91a5cbd9d2cb694715a0d89a12a5e6ea5ff417342c4bde798.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that:\n\n- For graph III, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, graph III shows a one-to-one function. As a result, this function is invertible.\n\n- For graphs I, II, and IV, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. These are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244150 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243442 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244150 1069 "q-108605" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244150 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244150 1071 "![](images/a6/a6e311063c3222d8be1b5fe37379d21c4dbb3198afd7c338a273bb1cf366fc1e.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244150 1072 #uuid "1b86aaf3-f04f-5a9d-8491-875b7ee69bad" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244150 1074 "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\nFrom the above, we see that:\n\n- For graph II, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, graph II shows a one-to-one function. As a result, this function is invertible.\n\n- For graphs I, III, and IV, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. These are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244151 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243637 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244151 1069 "q-108630" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244151 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244151 1071 "If the sum of the first $36$ terms of an arithmetic series is ${S}_{36}=\\frac{225}{2},$ and the last term is ${a}_{36}=\\frac{11}{2},$ what is the first term of the series?" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244151 1072 #uuid "7347742e-0cd7-5f1f-96ab-5609f6a91cbf" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244151 1074 "We are given the last term of our series. So, to find the first term, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series in the form\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{N}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, ${a}_{N}$ is the last term, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nSubstituting ${a}_{36}=\\frac{11}{2}$, $N=36$, and ${S}_{N}=\\frac{225}{2}$ into the formula above and solving for ${a}_{1}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{225}{2} & =\\frac{36}{2}({a}_{1}+\\frac{11}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{225}{36} & ={a}_{1}+\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ \\frac{25}{4} & ={a}_{1}+\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{25}{4}-\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244152 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243875 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244152 1069 "q-108635" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244152 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244152 1071 "What is the first term of an arithmetic series if the common difference is $d=0.6,$ and the sum of the first $37$ terms is ${S}_{37}=444?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244152 1072 #uuid "4e60b109-bf7a-518b-8abe-e7ea35536014" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244152 1074 "We are given the common difference of the series. So, to find the first term, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series in the form\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, $d$ is the common difference, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nSubstituting $N=37$, $d=0.6$, and ${S}_{N}=444$ into the formula above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}444 & =\\frac{37}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(37-1)(0.6)) \\\\ 888 & =37(2{a}_{1}+21.6) \\\\ \\frac{888}{37} & =2{a}_{1}+21.6 \\\\ 2.4 & =2{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =1.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244153 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243742 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244153 1069 "q-108637" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244153 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244153 1071 "What is the first term of an arithmetic series if the common difference is $d=\\frac{5}{7},$ and the sum of the first $50$ terms is ${S}_{50}=1\\,125?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244153 1072 #uuid "efe44e24-3349-54e3-ae25-0cd4ad9b52a1" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244153 1074 "We are given the common difference of the series. So, to find the first term, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series in the form\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, $d$ is the common difference, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nSubstituting $N=50$, $d=\\frac{5}{7}$, and ${S}_{N}=1\\,125$ into the formula above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\,125 & =\\frac{50}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(50-1)(\\frac{5}{7})) \\\\ 1\\,125 & =25(2{a}_{1}+35) \\\\ 45 & =2{a}_{1}+35 \\\\ 10 & =2{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244154 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243739 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244154 1069 "q-108640" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244154 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244154 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for ${x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)+4{y}^{4}=6{x}^{3}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244154 1072 #uuid "56e6a335-1c6d-5f20-b447-7c7858535bf5" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244154 1074 "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n Note that to differentiate the ${x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)+4{y}^{4}=6{x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)+4{y}^{4}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y))+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{y}^{4}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{x}^{3}) \\\\ [{x}^{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)]+16{y}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =18{x}^{2} \\\\ -{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)+16{y}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =18{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+16{y}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =18{x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(16{y}^{3}-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)) & =18{x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{18{x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)}{16{y}^{3}-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244155 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243583 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244155 1069 "q-108655" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244155 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244155 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $7{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+3{y}^{2}=12{x}^{2}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244155 1072 #uuid "82a0f4a1-b7fe-533e-9033-fcc9236329fd" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244155 1074 "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $7{x}^{3}{y}^{3}$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+3{y}^{2} & =12{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+3{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(12{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7{x}^{3}{y}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(12{x}^{2}) \\\\ [21{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+21{x}^{2}{y}^{3}]+6y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =24x \\\\ 21{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+21{x}^{2}{y}^{3}+6y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =24x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}21{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =24x-21{x}^{2}{y}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(21{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+6y) & =24x-21{x}^{2}{y}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{8x-7{x}^{2}{y}^{3}}{7{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244156 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243659 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244156 1069 "q-108667" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244156 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244156 1071 "![](images/81/811ead4928d2f245235736dce473d121261ea358ca4ff3a0cc8b01bb2e9f3b31.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-3,3)$\n- $x\\in [1,4]$\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,-3]$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244156 1072 #uuid "15f88305-1592-5719-98bf-9e054cd03fe2" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244156 1074 "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,-3].$ Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/32/32f1ad5bdec30951900a1c30c2996ea040e65880f859c43769faf374db06a2f0.png)\n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on the intervals $x\\in (-3,3)$ and $x\\in [1,4]$ because some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/8e/8ed451b24fa83de513f4a1b5c79d39f94add784c6b7265e765a3381e7b03363a.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244157 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243823 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244157 1069 "q-108673" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244157 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244157 1071 "![](images/3d/3db32ea943ff1f457704a29f325a592eb4e1e0e53ee258041d08346bf9da4716.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-2,0]$\n- $x\\in (-3,0)$\n- $x\\in (0,2)$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244157 1072 #uuid "35a0e892-6583-50a3-a3c1-b86c3920f28b" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244157 1074 "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The given function is invertible on the intervals $x\\in (-2,0]$ and $x\\in (0,2).$ Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/46/46e1daf2cb9025b6a736f223fbe168ea32b66302cebea23ca3a2d473e78b77b3.png)\n\n        \n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on the interval $(-3,0)$  because some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/d2/d2ed3670f7c67d60c7739ee35d66943b8341b2a49faa97546242609dd39afabc.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244158 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243600 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244158 1069 "q-108674" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244158 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244158 1071 "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,2)$ for the curve $y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)=1.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244158 1072 #uuid "ffb47def-243e-5613-87da-6b6ae8fdf163" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244158 1074 "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ y\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) & =0 \\\\ y(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,2)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(\\pi /3,\\,2)} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)})}_{(\\pi /3,\\,2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244159 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243604 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244159 1069 "q-108679" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244159 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244159 1071 "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(4,\\,4)$ for the curve $\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}=4.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244159 1072 #uuid "85cec444-2f0a-598c-bff4-89c6e3584a08" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244159 1074 "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/2}+{y}^{1/2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(4,\\,4)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(4,\\,4)} \\\\  & =-{\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt{x}}}_{(4,\\,4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{4}}{\\sqrt{4}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{2} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244160 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243806 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244160 1069 "q-1087" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244160 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244160 1071 "Calculate $det(T)$, if\n    $T=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 17 \\\\ 1 & 10\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244160 1072 #uuid "2c5d0d5a-1c35-5e42-acae-01a474314ae5" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244160 1074 "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}|=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 17 \\\\ 1 & 10\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10-17\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =5-17 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244161 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243755 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244161 1069 "q-108724" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244161 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244161 1071 "Find the first term of an arithmetic series if its $5$th term is $-0.28$ and the sum of the first $12$ terms of the series is $-12.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244161 1072 #uuid "fae49860-d008-5fe7-b59b-eb9baa184c01" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244161 1074 "We are given one term of the series. So, to find the first term, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series in the form\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, $d$ is the common difference, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nSubstituting $N=12$ and ${S}_{N}=-12$ into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ -12 & =\\frac{12}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(12-1)d) \\\\ -12 & =6(2{a}_{1}+11d) \\\\ -2 & =2{a}_{1}+11d.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we can write an equation for the $5$th term of the arithmetic series and substitute in its known value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\ {a}_{5} & ={a}_{1}+(5-1)d \\\\ -0.28 & ={a}_{1}+4d\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-0.28={a}_{1}+4d \\\\ -2=2{a}_{1}+11d\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we multiply the first equation by $-2$ and add the result to the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.56 & =-2{a}_{1}-8d \\\\ -2 & =2{a}_{1}+11d \\\\ -1.44 & =3d \\\\ d & =-0.48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $d=-0.48$ into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-0.28 & ={a}_{1}+4(-0.48) \\\\ -0.28 & ={a}_{1}-1.92 \\\\ {a}_{1} & =1.64.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244162 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243505 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244162 1069 "q-108735" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244162 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244162 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3x+6y.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=x+2y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244162 1072 #uuid "05430704-5a00-5ff9-8973-3229343f5e50" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244162 1074 "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3(x+2y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+2y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1) & =3z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=6z+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=6z+1.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244163 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243472 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244163 1069 "q-108736" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244163 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244163 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}+6xy+3{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=x+y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244163 1072 #uuid "10d103bc-2f56-5346-9216-0cbfc3385289" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244163 1074 "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3(x+y)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =3{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=3{z}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=3{z}^{2}+1.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244164 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243817 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244164 1069 "q-108744" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244164 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244164 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={tan}^{2}⁡(4x+4y).\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=x+y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244164 1072 #uuid "0fcc97e4-3645-5517-967a-e3b54a04fc40" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244164 1074 "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={tan}^{2}⁡(4(x+y)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & ={tan}^{2}⁡(4z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}={tan}^{2}⁡(4z)+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)={tan}^{2}⁡(4z)+1.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244165 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243873 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244165 1069 "q-108748" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244165 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244165 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{8x-12y+1}{12x-18y+3}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=2x-3y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244165 1072 #uuid "463a8e36-eaf0-59f2-9877-1d9a9eb456ab" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244165 1074 "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4(2x-3y)+1}{6(2x-3y)+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=2x-3y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2-3\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}(2-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}(2-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}) & =\\frac{4z+1}{6z+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2z+1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=\\frac{1}{2z+1}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244166 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243510 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244166 1069 "q-108750" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244166 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244166 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x+8y-1}{4}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=x+4y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244166 1072 #uuid "458fe83e-5ab5-56bf-a1ff-1862be912e82" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244166 1074 "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2(x+4y)-1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+4y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+4\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1) & =\\frac{2z-1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=2z.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244167 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243653 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244167 1069 "q-108751" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244167 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244167 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2+y-x}{x-y}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=x-y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244167 1072 #uuid "52822c39-606a-5a54-9e92-3a8ac02903e0" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244167 1074 "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2-(x-y)}{x-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x-y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2-z}{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2(z-1)}{z}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=\\frac{2(z-1)}{z}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244168 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243634 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244168 1069 "q-108775" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244168 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244168 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(x+y)^{2}+2(x+y)+1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(g(x,y))=x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244168 1072 #uuid "232cf02f-bc40-5c5e-8048-e19ed298d786" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244168 1074 "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(x+y+1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =(z+1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=(z+1)^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(z+1)^{2}+1}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{(z+1)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(z+1) & =x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+y+1)=x+C\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\ng(x,y)=x+y+1.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244169 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243556 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244169 1069 "q-108778" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244169 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244169 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4x+4y-1.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=x+y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244169 1072 #uuid "9a2671a6-1473-58e5-bd03-7325b2e118fc" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244169 1074 "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4(x+y)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =4z-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=4z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=4z.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244170 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243550 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244170 1069 "q-108780" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244170 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244170 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2{sin}^{2}⁡(3x+3y)-1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡(g(x,y))=-6x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244170 1072 #uuid "0b7b4d1c-df4d-5986-bef2-30931183e55d" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244170 1074 "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2{sin}^{2}⁡(3(x+y))-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =2{sin}^{2}⁡(3z)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2{sin}^{2}⁡(3z).\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{csc}^{2}⁡(3z)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =2 \\\\ \\int {csc}^{2}⁡(3z)\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3z) & =2x+K \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡(3z) & =-6x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-3K.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x+3y)=-6x+C\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\ng(x,y)=3x+3y.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244171 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243789 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244171 1069 "q-108791" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244171 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244171 1071 "![](images/4f/4fec1c187ae2eaaf40461775425b31d896bdc63bc99233277d6f6bac8949315d.png)\n\nDetermine the largest domain on which the function $f(x)$ (shown above) is invertible." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244171 1072 #uuid "c8649e7a-85fc-57ea-9a08-e015b963b698" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244171 1074 "First, notice that the domain of our function is $x\\in [-3,5].$ \n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ does not pass the horizontal line test on the interval $x\\in (1,3).$ In this case, the horizontal line $y=1$ intersects the graph at more than one point.\n\n![](images/eb/eb544795c61be7dd87568e6def10fc907c3554b603c3b344f88ac2a8ff847f8d.png)\n\n- Among the given options, the largest domain on which the function $f(x)$ is invertible is $x\\in [-3,1]\\cup (3,5].$ On this domain, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/40/4059a1ad684023640358af6ee38f0967957336578eb93b3299c9c842e7ce108f.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x\\in [-3,1]\\cup (3,5].$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244172 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243667 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244172 1069 "q-108795" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244172 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244172 1071 "![](images/de/def2afaad6d1f74ca3ac8833ed6e35efea1296b324ef5a14c2d9513983a63456.png)\n\nDetermine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)$ (shown above) is invertible." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244172 1072 #uuid "a8e69af0-0327-53e8-9129-272172fb2aff" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244172 1074 "First, notice that the domain of our function is $x\\in [-2,4].$\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ does not pass the horizontal line test on the interval $x\\in [-2,0].$ In this case, the horizontal line $y=-2$ intersects the graph at more than one point.\n\n![](images/03/033b9e827caf4fc89d4b179c769f5115254c811953f93caf33d0959bfd063235.png)\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the interval $x\\in [0,4].$ On this interval, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/4c/4c91aa95131758065b1d8befe7c69895dc51d2f984a04cc8f06932a32e552792.png)\n\nTherefore, the largest interval on which the function is invertible is:\n\n$$\nx\\in [0,4]\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244173 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243752 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244173 1069 "q-108800" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244173 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244173 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={cos}^{2}⁡(2x-y+1).\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=2x-y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244173 1072 #uuid "0f19af75-a0de-5eec-8c80-0e4cc891ad39" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244173 1074 "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=2x-y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & ={cos}^{2}⁡(z+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2-{cos}^{2}⁡(z+1).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=2-{cos}^{2}⁡(z+1).$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244174 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243756 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244174 1069 "q-108801" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244174 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244174 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x+y}{x+y+2}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=x+y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244174 1072 #uuid "c1e5da0c-2052-5523-9a17-9e4db44937d8" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244174 1074 "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =\\frac{z}{z+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{z}{z+2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{2z+2}{z+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(z+1)}{z+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=\\frac{2(z+1)}{z+2}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244175 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243464 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244175 1069 "q-108802" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244175 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244175 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={tan}^{2}⁡(3x-5y).\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=3x-5y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244175 1072 #uuid "e81268a9-ddfd-5f92-b961-b0aa841e7817" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244175 1074 "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=3x-5y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=3-5\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & ={tan}^{2}⁡z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=3-5{tan}^{2}⁡z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=3-5{tan}^{2}⁡z.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244176 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243720 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244176 1069 "q-108804" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244176 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244176 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4-\\sqrt{y-2x+3}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=y-2x,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244176 1072 #uuid "81e15a4c-8648-5f23-bf1b-4b77ab33f425" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244176 1074 "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=y-2x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2 & =4-\\sqrt{z+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2-\\sqrt{z+3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=2-\\sqrt{z+3}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244177 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243595 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244177 1069 "q-108806" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244177 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244177 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(5y-7x+2)^{5}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=5y-7x,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244177 1072 #uuid "51067b45-e2b0-53c6-a828-7ca17b7579fd" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244177 1074 "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=5y-7x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-7\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{7}{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{7}{5} & =(z+2)^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=5(z+2)^{5}-7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=5(z+2)^{5}-7.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244178 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243841 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244178 1069 "q-108819" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244178 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244178 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5x+5y)-1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|g(x,y)|=10x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244178 1072 #uuid "e58752d6-0044-55ba-a578-6076003cbc1e" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244178 1074 "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5(x+y))-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5z)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5z).\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(5z)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =2 \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cot}⁡(5z)\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5z)| & =2x+K \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5z)| & =10x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=5K.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+5y)|=10x+C\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\ng(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+5y).\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244179 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243852 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244179 1069 "q-108821" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244179 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244179 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{e}^{2}}{(x+y)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=x+y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244179 1072 #uuid "f9f22a63-9e10-5f11-a18c-a10e6e359539" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244179 1074 "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}+1.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244180 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243592 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244180 1069 "q-10883" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244180 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244180 1071 "Find ${f}^{″}(x)$ for $f(x)=4\\sqrt{x}+5{x}^{2}+x.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244180 1072 #uuid "2dffb4d9-bf1b-5b2c-8ebe-2cdd8aa443db" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244180 1074 "Computing the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(4\\sqrt{x}+5{x}^{2}+x)}^{′} \\\\  & ={(4{x}^{1/2}+5{x}^{2}+x)}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4{x}^{-1/2}+2\\cdot 5x+1 \\\\  & =2{x}^{-1/2}+10x+1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & ={(2{x}^{-1/2}+10x+1)}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2{x}^{-3/2}+10+0 \\\\  & =-{x}^{-3/2}+10 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}+10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244181 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243870 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244181 1069 "q-10884" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244181 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244181 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for $y=3{x}^{5}+4{x}^{3}-10{x}^{2}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244181 1072 #uuid "c88b9b5e-25b3-5b4b-a09e-22dcd4394553" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244181 1074 "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{5}+4{x}^{3}-10{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 3{x}^{4}+3\\cdot 4{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 10x \\\\  & =15{x}^{4}+12{x}^{2}-20x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(15{x}^{4}+12{x}^{2}-20x) \\\\  & =15\\cdot 4{x}^{3}+2\\cdot 12x-20 \\\\  & =60{x}^{3}+24x-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244182 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243459 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244182 1069 "q-108840" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244182 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244182 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(x+y+2)^{3}-1.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=C-2x,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244182 1072 #uuid "c93f6fd2-747e-585d-8694-c18e2cf8aef0" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244182 1074 "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =(z+2)^{3}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=(z+2)^{3}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(z+2)^{3}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{(z+2)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2(z+2)^{2}} & =x+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{(z+2)^{2}} & =C-2x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-2K.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(x+y+2)^{2}}=C-2x\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y)=\\frac{1}{(x+y+2)^{2}}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244183 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243741 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244183 1069 "q-108841" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244183 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244183 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{e}^{x+y}-x-y}{x+y}.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=C-x,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244183 1072 #uuid "a1afbf5f-1cd8-5fe7-a308-11eeef08401b" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244183 1074 "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =\\frac{{e}^{z}-z}{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{e}^{z}}{z}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{z}{{e}^{z}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int z{e}^{-z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing integration by parts on the left side,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=z & \\,⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{-z} & \\,⟹\\,v=-{e}^{-z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int z{e}^{-z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -z{e}^{-z}+\\int {e}^{-z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -z{e}^{-z}-{e}^{-z} & =x+C \\\\ -{e}^{-z}(z+1) & =x+K \\\\ {e}^{-z}(z+1) & =C-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-K.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n{e}^{-x-y}(x+y+1)=C-x\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y)={e}^{-x-y}(x+y+1).$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244184 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243745 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244184 1069 "q-108849" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244184 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244184 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-2y).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=6x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244184 1072 #uuid "a51e1b09-60f2-55a7-aabb-d874844edbe0" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244184 1074 "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2(x-y))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x-y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2z).\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2z)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =3 \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2z)\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int 3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2z) & =3x+K \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2z) & =6x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=2K.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-2y)=6x+C\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\ng(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-2y).\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244185 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243633 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244185 1069 "q-108854" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244185 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244185 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4x+2y}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|g(x,y)|=4y-C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244185 1072 #uuid "ec13409a-5c91-558a-9049-5e58f2cbc2ec" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244185 1074 "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2(2x+y)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=2x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2 & =\\frac{1}{2z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4z+1}{2z}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2z}{4z+1}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{2z}{4z+1}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\int (1-\\frac{1}{4z+1})\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(z-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4z+1|) & =x+K \\\\ 4z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4z+1| & =8x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=8K.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x+4y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|8x+4y+1| & =8x+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|8x+4y+1| & =4y-C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\ng(x,y)=8x+4y+1.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244186 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243492 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244186 1069 "q-108855" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244186 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244186 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{(x-2y)^{2}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y)}.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=C-x,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244186 1072 #uuid "8e48cb80-8431-5480-8902-5252c215795f" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244186 1074 "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x-2y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1-2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{z}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing integration by parts on the left side,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡z & \\,⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{z} \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}} & \\,⟹\\,v=-\\frac{1}{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}{z}+\\int \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}{z}-\\frac{1}{z} & =x+C \\\\ -\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+1}{z} & =x+K \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+1}{z} & =C-x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-K.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y)+1}{x-2y}=C-x\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y)+1}{x-2y}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244187 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243504 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244187 1069 "q-108856" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244187 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244187 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\sqrt{x+y}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)}-1.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=\\frac{x}{2}+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244187 1072 #uuid "7e4fa5d6-f68a-5f6e-a659-23ced434a09f" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244187 1074 "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =\\frac{\\sqrt{z}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\sqrt{z}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}{\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}{\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing integration by parts on the left side,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡z & \\,⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{z} \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z}} & \\,⟹\\,v=2\\sqrt{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}{\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 2\\sqrt{z}\\operatorname{ln}⁡z-2\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 2\\sqrt{z}\\operatorname{ln}⁡z-4\\sqrt{z} & =x+K \\\\ 2\\sqrt{z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡z-2) & =x+K \\\\ \\sqrt{z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡z-2) & =\\frac{x}{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=\\frac{K}{2}.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x+y}\\,(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2) & =\\frac{x}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y)=\\sqrt{x+y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2).$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244188 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243761 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244188 1069 "q-108857" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244188 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244188 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{9x-3y}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|g(x,y)|=y+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244188 1072 #uuid "5a92b889-cf95-57c9-b2d1-07f0f25b0f57" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244188 1074 "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{3(3x-y)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=3x-y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=3-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{3z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{9z-2}{3z}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3z}{9z-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{3z}{9z-2}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\int (1+\\frac{2}{9z-2})\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(z+\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|9z-2|) & =x+K \\\\ z+\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|9z-2| & =3x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3K.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-y+\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|27x-9y-2| & =3x+C \\\\ \\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|27x-9y-2| & =y+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\ng(x,y)=27x-9y-2.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244189 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243622 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244189 1069 "q-108858" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244189 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244189 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{(2x+y)\\,{2}^{2x+y}}-2.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244189 1072 #uuid "fe2488e6-1056-5c66-a150-25aaef8df7c0" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244189 1074 "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=2x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2 & =\\frac{1}{z\\,{2}^{z}}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{z\\,{2}^{z}}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\,{2}^{z}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int z\\,{2}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing integration by parts on the left side,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=z & \\,⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={2}^{z} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\frac{{2}^{z}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int z\\,{2}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{z\\,{2}^{z}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int {2}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{z\\,{2}^{z}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(\\frac{{2}^{z}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}) & =x+C \\\\ \\frac{{2}^{z}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(z-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}) & =x+K \\\\ {2}^{z}(z-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}) & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=K\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2x+y}(2x+y-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}) & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y)={2}^{2x+y}(2x+y-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}).$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244190 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243635 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244190 1069 "q-108891" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244190 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244190 1071 "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:\\frac{x+2}{3}=\\frac{y}{4}=\\frac{1-z}{2},\\,Π:r\\cdot ⟨4,-3,1⟩=-9.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244190 1072 #uuid "7ed76edb-2aee-5101-83be-e4a973b64993" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244190 1074 "From the equation of the line $l$, we know that it passes through the point $A(-2,0,1)$ and it is parallel to the vector $v=⟨3,4,-2⟩.$ Therefore, its vector equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨-2,0,1⟩+t⟨3,4,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2+3t,4t,1-2t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, substituting $r=⟨-2+3t,4t,1-2t⟩$ into the equation of the plane, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot ⟨4,-3,1⟩ & =-9 \\\\ ⟨-2+3t,4t,1-2t⟩\\cdot ⟨4,-3,1⟩ & =-9 \\\\ 4(-2+3t)-3(4t)+(1-2t) & =-9 \\\\ -8+12t-12t+1-2t & =-9 \\\\ -2t & =-2 \\\\ t & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to get the position vector $p$ of the intersection point $P,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =⟨-2+3(1),4(1),1-2(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,4,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get $P(1,4,-1).$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244191 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243461 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244191 1069 "q-108898" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244191 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244191 1071 "The first term of an arithmetic series is ${a}_{1}=1,$ the common difference is $d=\\frac{1}{2},$ and the sum of the series is ${S}_{N}=52.$ Find the number of terms $N$ in the series." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244191 1072 #uuid "800f0768-2cd2-5060-956e-d2760762a123" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244191 1074 "To find the number of terms, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d).\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${a}_{1}=1,$ $d=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${S}_{N}=52$ into the above and solve for $N$, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 52 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2\\cdot 1+(N-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ 104 & =N(2+\\frac{N}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ 104 & =\\frac{{N}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}N \\\\ {N}^{2}+3N-208 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the equation above, the quadratic formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{{3}^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot (-208)}}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{841}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm 29}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $N=13$ or $N=-16.$ We disregard the negative solution since $N$ must be positive.\n\nTherefore, our series has $N=13$ terms." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244192 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243724 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244192 1069 "q-108900" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244192 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244192 1071 "The first term of an arithmetic series is ${a}_{1}=5,$ the common difference is $d=-2.5,$ and the sum of the series is ${S}_{N}=-105.$ Find the number of terms $N$ in the series." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244192 1072 #uuid "402b8fa5-387a-5b0d-a34b-55ae3fbdf7b5" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244192 1074 "To find the number of terms, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d).\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${a}_{1}=5$, $d=-2.5$ and ${S}_{N}=-105$ into the above and solve for $N$, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ -105 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2\\cdot 5+(N-1)\\cdot (-2.5)) \\\\ -210 & =N(10-2.5N+2.5) \\\\ -210 & =12.5N-2.5{N}^{2} \\\\ 2.5{N}^{2}-12.5N-210 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the equation above, the quadratic formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N & =\\frac{-(-12.5)\\pm \\sqrt{(-12.5)^{2}-4\\cdot 2.5\\cdot (-210)}}{2\\cdot 2.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{12.5\\pm 47.5}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $N=12$ or $N=-7.$ We disregard the negative solution since $N$ must be positive.\n\nTherefore, our series has $N=12$ terms." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244193 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243588 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244193 1069 "q-108902" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244193 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244193 1071 "Calculate the number of terms in the following arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n6+9+12+…=195\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244193 1072 #uuid "7dd23595-5ea3-57db-9364-6bcd14012465" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244193 1074 "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series:\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d)\\,\\,\n$$\n\nWe have been given the sum ${S}_{N}=195,$ and the first term ${a}_{1}=6.$ Our goal is to find $N,$ the number of terms.\n\nThe common difference $d$ can be found, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =9-6 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 195 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2(6)+(N-1)(3)) \\\\ 390 & =N(12+3N-3) \\\\ 390 & =N(9+3N) \\\\ 390 & =3{N}^{2}+9N \\\\ 3{N}^{2}+9N-390 & =0 \\\\ {N}^{2}+3N-130 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the equation above, the quadratic formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{{3}^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot (-130)}}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{529}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm 23}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $N=10$ or $N=-13.$ We disregard the negative solution since $N$ must be positive.\n\nTherefore, our series has $N=10$ terms." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244194 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243811 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244194 1069 "q-108904" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244194 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244194 1071 "Calculate the number of terms in the following arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n2+2.5+3+…=470\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244194 1072 #uuid "631970bc-7173-5c6f-8c08-2b5a8924de2b" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244194 1074 "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series:\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d)\\,\\,\n$$\n\nWe have been given the sum ${S}_{N}=470,$ and the first term ${a}_{1}=2.$ Our goal is to find $N,$ the number of terms.\n\nThe common difference $d$ can be found, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =2.5-2 \\\\  & =0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 470 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2(2)+(N-1)(0.5)) \\\\ 940 & =N(4+0.5N-0.5) \\\\ 940 & =N(0.5N+3.5) \\\\ 940 & =0.5{N}^{2}+3.5N \\\\ 0.5{N}^{2}+3.5N-940 & =0 \\\\ {N}^{2}+7N-1\\,880 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the equation above, the quadratic formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N & =\\frac{-7\\pm \\sqrt{(-7)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1\\,880)}}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-7\\pm \\sqrt{7\\,569}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-7\\pm 87}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $N=40$ or $N=-47.$ We disregard the negative solution since $N$ must be positive.\n\nTherefore, our series has $N=40$ terms." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244195 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243508 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244195 1069 "q-108909" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244195 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244195 1071 "Calculate the number of terms in the following arithmetic series. \n\n$$\n4.2+4.7+5.2+...=139.4\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244195 1072 #uuid "a6487f23-2be0-5df1-92f0-34bd73e7850d" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244195 1074 "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series:\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d)\n$$\n\nWe have been given the sum ${S}_{N}=139.4,$ and the first term ${a}_{1}=4.2.$ Our goal is to find $N,$ the number of terms.\n\nThe common difference $d$ can be found, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =4.7-4.2 \\\\  & =0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula for ${S}_{N}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 139.4 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2(4.2)+(N-1)(0.5)) \\\\ 278.8 & =N(8.4+0.5N-0.5) \\\\ 278.8 & =N(7.9+0.5N) \\\\ 278.8 & =7.9N+0.5{N}^{2} \\\\ 0.5{N}^{2}+7.9N-278.8 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the equation above, the quadratic formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N & =\\frac{-7.9\\pm \\sqrt{(-7.9)^{2}-4\\cdot 0.5\\cdot (-278.8)}}{2\\cdot 0.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{-7.9\\pm 24.9}{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $N=17$ or $N=-32.8.$ We disregard the negative solution since $N$ must be positive and integer.\n\nTherefore, our series has $N=17$ terms." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244196 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243641 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244196 1069 "q-108910" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244196 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244196 1071 "Calculate the number of terms in the following arithmetic series. \n\n$$\n5.5+6+6.5+...=77.5\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244196 1072 #uuid "704e846f-c210-5c5d-a407-a664f26d21be" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244196 1074 "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series:\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d)\n$$\n\nWe have been given the sum ${S}_{N}=77.5,$ and the first term ${a}_{1}=5.5.$ Our goal is to find $N,$ the number of terms.\n\nThe common difference $d$ can be found, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =6-5.5 \\\\  & =0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula for ${S}_{N}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 77.5 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2(5.5)+(N-1)(0.5)) \\\\ 155 & =N(11+0.5N-0.5) \\\\ 155 & =N(10.5+0.5N) \\\\ 155 & =10.5N+0.5{N}^{2} \\\\ 0.5{N}^{2}+10.5N-155 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the equation above, the quadratic formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N & =\\frac{-10.5\\pm \\sqrt{(-10.5)^{2}-4\\cdot 0.5\\cdot (-155)}}{2\\cdot 0.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10.5\\pm 20.5}{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $N=10$ or $N=-31.$ We disregard the negative solution since $N$ must be positive.\n\nTherefore, our series has $N=10$ terms." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244197 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243682 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244197 1069 "q-108911" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244197 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244197 1071 "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-\\sqrt{x}\\,y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{y}$\n- ${y}^{′}+y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}$ $$\n- ${y}^{′}-{y}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x={y}^{6}$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244197 1072 #uuid "f464429f-4905-5c37-b6cf-d2ce0cb09dd7" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244197 1074 "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-\\sqrt{x},\\,q(x)={x}^{3},\\,n=-1.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,\\,q(x)=-\\sqrt{x},\\,n=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $p(x)y$ term.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244198 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243621 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244198 1069 "q-108913" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244198 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244198 1071 "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{-2}{y}^{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$\n- ${y}^{′}-{e}^{x}y={x}^{2}{y}^{5}$\n- ${y}^{′}+{y}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=-x{y}^{3}$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244198 1072 #uuid "bc73c612-2a32-5b85-8989-8b463754a48c" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244198 1074 "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a real number.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing both the $p(x)y$ and $q(x){y}^{n}$ terms.\n\n- Equation II is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-{e}^{x},\\,q(x)={x}^{2},\\,n=5.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. It is missing the $p(x)y$ term.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"