045585 true] [17592186244210 1069 "q-108964" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244210 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244210 1071 "${J}_{2}(\\sqrt{2})=$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244210 1072 #uuid "26d6e8df-4e91-5449-ab96-853b9a0c76b2" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244210 1074 "Let's construct our matrix:\n\n- The index $2$ in ${J}_{2}(\\sqrt{2})$ means that we have a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\n- The value $\\sqrt{2}$ in ${J}_{2}(\\sqrt{2})$ means that we have $\\sqrt{2}$'s on the main diagonal.\n\n- Also, we must have $1$'s on the superdiagonal (the diagonal above the main one).\n\nGiven that all the other entries must be zero, our Jordan block is\n\n$$\n{J}_{2}(\\sqrt{2})=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244211 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243889 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244211 1069 "q-108966" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244211 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244211 1071 "${J}_{3}(-2)=$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244211 1072 #uuid "bc40ccb8-aaa3-5fb8-86c6-cb93a899b86d" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244211 1074 "Let's construct our matrix:\n\n- The index $3$ in ${J}_{3}(-2)$ means that we have a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n- The value $-2$ in ${J}_{3}(-2)$ means that we have $-2$'s on the main diagonal.\n\n- Also, we must have $1$'s on the superdiagonal (the diagonal above the main one).\n\nGiven that all the other entries must be zero, our Jordan block is\n\n$$\n{J}_{3}(-2)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244212 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243506 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244212 1069 "q-108967" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244212 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244212 1071 "${J}_{2}(\\pi )=$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244212 1072 #uuid "4a947abe-ba7a-566c-93a4-a47ed343ac3d" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244212 1074 "Let's construct our matrix:\n\n- The index $2$ in ${J}_{2}(\\pi )$ means that we have a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\n- The value $\\pi $ in ${J}_{2}(\\pi )$ means that we have $\\pi $'s on the main diagonal.\n\n- Also, we must have $1$'s on the superdiagonal (the diagonal above the main one).\n\nGiven that all the other entries must be zero, our Jordan block is\n\n$$\n{J}_{2}(\\pi )=[\\begin{aligned}\\pi  & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\pi \\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244213 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243882 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244213 1069 "q-108969" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244213 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244213 1071 "${J}_{3}(-8)=$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244213 1072 #uuid "40746efc-1bd0-5b5a-8d80-9e4bee243c60" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244213 1074 "Let's construct our matrix:\n\n- The index $3$ in ${J}_{3}(-8)$ means that we have a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n- The value $-8$ in ${J}_{3}(-8)$ means that we have $-8$'s on the main diagonal.\n\n- Also, we must have $1$'s on the superdiagonal (the diagonal above the main one).\n\nGiven that all the other entries must be zero, our Jordan block is\n\n$$\n{J}_{3}(-8)=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243497 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244214 1069 "q-108982" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244214 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244214 1071 "${J}_{1}(\\sqrt{3})\\,⊕\\,{J}_{3}(5)=$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244214 1072 #uuid "99797f36-8439-5a80-bb6d-eda0649b3172" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244214 1074 "First, recall the following:\n\n- ${J}_{1}(\\sqrt{3})$ represents a $1\\times 1$ Jordan block with $\\sqrt{3}$'s on the leading diagonal.\n\n- ${J}_{3}(5)$ represents a $3\\times 3$ Jordan block with $5$'s on the leading diagonal,\n\nTherefore, our Jordan blocks are given by\n\n$$\n{J}_{1}(\\sqrt{3})=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{J}_{3}(5)=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe composition of our Jordan blocks gives the following block diagonal matrix:\n\n$$\n{J}_{1}(\\sqrt{3})\\,⊕\\,{J}_{3}(5)=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244215 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243854 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244215 1069 "q-108983" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244215 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244215 1071 "${J}_{2}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-1)=$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244215 1072 #uuid "cb7274e4-7a6d-5bc2-8cf0-4191bb6c526d" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244215 1074 "First, recall the following:\n\n- ${J}_{2}(1)$ represents a $2\\times 2$ Jordan block with $1$'s on the leading diagonal.\n\n- ${J}_{1}(-1)$ represents a $1\\times 1$ Jordan block with $-1$'s on the leading diagonal,\n\nTherefore, our Jordan blocks are given by\n\n$$\n{J}_{2}(1)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{J}_{1}(-1)=[\\begin{aligned}-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe composition of our Jordan blocks gives the following block diagonal matrix:\n\n$$\n{J}_{2}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-1)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244216 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243795 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244216 1069 "q-109009" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244216 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244216 1071 "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some integer $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}-y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x={y}^{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $Q(x)?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244216 1072 #uuid "ac82a1e5-f377-51b8-8786-06db64bfa2d6" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244216 1074 "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,\\,q(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,n=5.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{5},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-5}\\frac{dy}{dx}-{y}^{-4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-4}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-4{y}^{-5}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-5}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-z\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+4z\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x)=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244217 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243849 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244217 1069 "q-109014" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244217 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244217 1071 "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some integer $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+3xy=-{x}^{4}{y}^{3}\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $P(x)?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244217 1072 #uuid "59ec66ad-ab07-5df0-ac9e-4d8b85fb44fb" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244217 1074 "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=3x,\\,q(x)=-{x}^{4},\\,n=3.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{3},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-3}\\frac{dy}{dx}+3x{y}^{-2}=-{x}^{4}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-2{y}^{-3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+3xz=-{x}^{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-6xz=2{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=-6x.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244218 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243715 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244218 1069 "q-109018" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244218 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244218 1071 "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some real number $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{4}y=\\sqrt{y}\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $P(x)?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244218 1072 #uuid "1fd9f6f1-19c3-5312-8ab5-3e759ad7a9dd" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244218 1074 "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{4},\\,q(x)=1,\\,n=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{1/2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-1/2}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{4}{y}^{1/2}=1.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{1/2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{4}z=1\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}z=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244219 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243709 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244219 1069 "q-10902" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244219 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244219 1071 "Find ${f}^{″}(x)$ for $f(x)={x}^{6}+{x}^{5}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244219 1072 #uuid "4b52b494-b991-5987-a3d3-0817f181b4b9" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244219 1074 "Computing the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={({x}^{6}+{x}^{5})}^{′} \\\\  & =6\\cdot {x}^{5}+5\\cdot {x}^{4} \\\\  & =6{x}^{5}+5{x}^{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & ={(6{x}^{5}+5{x}^{4})}^{′} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 6{x}^{4}+4\\cdot 5{x}^{3} \\\\  & =30{x}^{4}+20{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244220 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243853 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244220 1069 "q-109021" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244220 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244220 1071 "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some real number $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+\\frac{y}{x}={x}^{3}{y}^{3/4}\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $Q(x)?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244220 1072 #uuid "97e8bc76-2e14-55a4-986a-6a5e78385db7" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244220 1074 "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,q(x)={x}^{3},\\,n=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{3/4},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-3/4}\\frac{dy}{dx}+\\frac{{y}^{1/4}}{x}={x}^{3}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{1/4}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{-3/4}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-3/4}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n4\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}\\,z={x}^{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{4x}\\,z=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244221 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243714 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244221 1069 "q-109022" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244221 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244221 1071 "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some real number $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{y}}\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $P(x)?$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244221 1072 #uuid "59b54a0e-f75c-5a06-aa23-4e21a8ecaf83" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244221 1074 "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{2},\\,q(x)={x}^{3},\\,n=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the differential equation by ${y}^{1/2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{1/2}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}{y}^{3/2}={x}^{3}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{3/2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{1/2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{2}\\,z={x}^{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}\\,z=\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244222 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243732 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244222 1069 "q-109026" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244222 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244222 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy=x{y}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244222 1072 #uuid "589d9965-97b7-5fc2-a133-6802310284c2" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244222 1074 "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=x,\\,q(x)=x,\\,n=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-2}\\frac{dy}{dx}+x{y}^{-1}=x.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+xz=x\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-xz=-x\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=-x,\\,Q(x)=-x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-xz) & ={e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}\\cdot (-x) \\\\ {e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-xz{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2} & =-x{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}) & =-x{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2})\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int x{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2} & ={e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}+C \\\\ z & =1+C{e}^{{x}^{2}/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1}=1+C{e}^{{x}^{2}/2}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{{x}^{2}/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244223 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243727 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244223 1069 "q-109027" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244223 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244223 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{x}^{2}y={x}^{2}{y}^{-4}.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244223 1072 #uuid "dc20c4d3-6f46-5d1b-920a-dfd533b0b1fc" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244223 1074 "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-3{x}^{2},\\,q(x)={x}^{2},\\,n=-4.\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the differential equation by ${y}^{4},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{4}\\frac{dy}{dx}-3{x}^{2}{y}^{5}={x}^{2}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{5}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=5{y}^{4}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-3{x}^{2}z={x}^{2}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-15{x}^{2}z=5{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=-15{x}^{2},\\,Q(x)=5{x}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-15{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-5{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={e}^{-5{x}^{3}}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-5{x}^{3}}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-15{x}^{2}z) & ={e}^{-5{x}^{3}}\\cdot (5{x}^{2}) \\\\ {e}^{-5{x}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-15{x}^{2}z{e}^{-5{x}^{3}} & =5{x}^{2}{e}^{-5{x}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-5{x}^{3}}) & =5{x}^{2}{e}^{-5{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-5{x}^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 5{x}^{2}{e}^{-5{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{e}^{-5{x}^{3}} & =-\\frac{{e}^{-5{x}^{3}}}{3}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C{e}^{5{x}^{3}}-1}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{5}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{5}=\\frac{C{e}^{5{x}^{3}}-1}{3}\\,⇒\\,y=\\sqrt[5]{\\frac{C{e}^{5{x}^{3}}-1}{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244224 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243443 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244224 1069 "q-109028" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244224 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244224 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=-x{y}^{3}.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244224 1072 #uuid "58944fcc-b27a-50b8-9784-20e0e51f15da" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244224 1074 "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=1,\\,q(x)=-x,\\,n=3.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{3},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-3}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{y}^{-2}=-x.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-2{y}^{-3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z=-x\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2z=2x\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=-2,\\,Q(x)=2x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-2)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={e}^{-2x}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2z) & ={e}^{-2x}\\cdot (2x) \\\\ {e}^{-2x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2z{e}^{-2x} & =2x{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-2x}) & =2x{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-2x})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2x{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{e}^{-2x} & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2x}(2x+1)+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C{e}^{2x}-2x-1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=2{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-2}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-2}=\\frac{C{e}^{2x}-2x-1}{2}\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{C{e}^{2x}-2x-1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244225 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243673 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244225 1069 "q-109029" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244225 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244225 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y=\\sqrt{y}.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244225 1072 #uuid "f2cab3ea-07f6-5b6d-87cc-2af665f5707a" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244225 1074 "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-1,\\,q(x)=1,\\,n=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{1/2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-1/2}\\frac{dy}{dx}-{y}^{1/2}=1.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{1/2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-z=1\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{2}z=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-1/2)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={e}^{-x/2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x/2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{2}z) & ={e}^{-x/2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ {e}^{-x/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{2}z{e}^{-x/2} & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x/2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-x/2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-x/2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{e}^{-x/2} & =-{e}^{-x/2}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =C{e}^{x/2}-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C={C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{1/2}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{1/2}=C{e}^{x/2}-1\\,⇒\\,y={(C{e}^{x/2}-1)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244226 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243831 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244226 1069 "q-109057" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244226 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244226 1071 "${J}_{1}(0)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{2}(2)=$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244226 1072 #uuid "5a6d42e8-0278-5400-927a-d4ffe8aa9b4e" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244226 1074 "First, recall the following:\n\n- ${J}_{1}(0)$ represents a $1\\times 1$ Jordan block with $0$'s on the leading diagonal,\n\n- ${J}_{2}(2)$ represents a $2\\times 2$ Jordan block with $2$'s on the leading diagonal.\n\nTherefore, our Jordan blocks are given by\n\n$$\n{J}_{1}(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0\\end{aligned}],\\,{J}_{2}(2)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe composition of our Jordan blocks gives the following block diagonal matrix:\n\n$$\n{J}_{1}(0)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{2}(2)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244227 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243654 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244227 1069 "q-109058" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244227 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244227 1071 "${J}_{1}(-7)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{2}(-2)=$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244227 1072 #uuid "604e7108-f531-50e7-8989-ba9c66317aab" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244227 1074 "First, recall the following:\n\n- ${J}_{1}(-7)$ represents a $1\\times 1$ Jordan block with $-7$'s on the leading diagonal,\n\n- ${J}_{2}(-2)$ represents a $2\\times 2$ Jordan block with $-2$'s on the leading diagonal.\n\nTherefore, our Jordan blocks are given by\n\n$$\n{J}_{1}(-7)=[\\begin{aligned}-7\\end{aligned}],\\,{J}_{2}(-2)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe composition of our Jordan blocks gives the following block diagonal matrix:\n\n$$\n{J}_{1}(-7)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{2}(-2)=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244228 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243856 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244228 1069 "q-109067" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244228 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244228 1071 "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix above. Find its decomposition using Jordan blocks." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244228 1072 #uuid "5f1e2e3b-aca4-5a0d-9c09-7f84fb95a5d6" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244228 1074 "Our matrix can be represented as a single Jordan block:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]={J}_{3}(5)\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244229 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243471 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244229 1069 "q-109068" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244229 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244229 1071 "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}e & 1 \\\\ 0 & e\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix above. Find its decomposition using Jordan blocks." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244229 1072 #uuid "6eb0563a-013e-574e-bb9a-87a8858f764f" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244229 1074 "Our matrix can be represented as a single Jordan block:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}e & 1 \\\\ 0 & e\\end{aligned}]={J}_{2}(e)\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244230 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243545 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244230 1069 "q-109071" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244230 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244230 1071 "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix above. Find its decomposition using Jordan blocks." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244230 1072 #uuid "6cf2e4ce-753e-5cc2-bbcd-13c55bf15e1e" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244230 1074 "Our matrix can be represented as the composition of two Jordan blocks (one $3\\times 3$ block and one $1\\times 1$ block), as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]={J}_{3}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-5)\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244231 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243746 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244231 1069 "q-109075" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244231 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244231 1071 "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix above. Find its decomposition using Jordan blocks." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244231 1072 #uuid "a4cb3c16-a9bb-564f-9af9-5fe47bea0e92" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244231 1074 "Our matrix can be represented as the composition of two Jordan blocks (one $1\\times 1$ block and one $2\\times 2$ block), as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]={J}_{1}(3)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{2}(9)\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244232 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243690 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244232 1069 "q-109080" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244232 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244232 1071 "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix above. Find its decomposition using Jordan blocks." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244232 1072 #uuid "959e5e93-19ac-5875-9ad8-5db67a13ba59" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244232 1074 "Our matrix can be represented as the composition of three Jordan blocks (two $1\\times 1$ blocks and one $2\\times 2$ block), as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]={J}_{1}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{2}(-4)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(6)\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244233 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243666 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244233 1069 "q-109090" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244233 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244233 1071 "The probability distribution of the random variable $X$ is given below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=0,$ calculate $\\text{Var}[\\sqrt{5}X+4].$ \n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{3}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ |" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244233 1072 #uuid "7bc237e8-7011-5d1b-897c-e565fd160016" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244233 1074 "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constants $a$ and $b,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+b]={a}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\sqrt{5}$ and $b=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\sqrt{5}X+4] & ={(\\sqrt{5})}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the variance of $X$ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we're given that $\\text{E}[X]=0.$ \n\nWe start by computing $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$ Summing up the products of each value of ${X}^{2}$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & =(-1)^{2}\\cdot f(-1)+{0}^{2}\\cdot f(0)+{1}^{2}\\cdot f(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}-{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\sqrt{5}X+4] & =5\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244234 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243877 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244234 1069 "q-1091" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244234 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244234 1071 "Calculate the minor of the entry ${x}_{21}$ in the matrix\n$X=[\\begin{aligned}9 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 8 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 11\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244234 1072 #uuid "e8368bac-9938-5997-ba50-764e8ba7ddb9" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244234 1074 "The minor of ${x}_{21}=-2$ (second row, first column) is the determinant of the remaining entries when the column and row containing $-2$ are removed: \n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}∗ & -3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & 0 & 11\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of ${x}_{21}=-2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 11\\end{aligned}] & =|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 11\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 11-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-33-0 \\\\  & =-33.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244235 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243606 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244235 1069 "q-109102" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244235 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244235 1071 "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244235 1072 #uuid "c401ac87-eb9d-550f-aa95-5fff16627f53" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244235 1074 "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )-1\\cdot (-4) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(1)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244236 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243760 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244236 1069 "q-109111" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244236 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244236 1071 "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(-1,0),(1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+3xy\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244236 1072 #uuid "538aef16-d53a-586e-bc6f-7e8857a93b64" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244236 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/0d/0dfe132146c4095954d6b628b055317c975fed6ec429b9ad48b68f4015d6c033.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=3xy.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+xy\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3y-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=y.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244237 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243678 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244237 1069 "q-109112" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244237 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244237 1071 "Consider the rectangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0),(2,0),(2,1)$ and $(0,1),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244237 1072 #uuid "c38c694f-327f-51b0-bb98-98b827fdfa0a" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244237 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/8d/8d57fb7f6b8e2e822a11f11a0267994c449c37b43df167a8d48ab8036854b8dd.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{x}^{2})]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)={x}^{2}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244238 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243862 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244238 1069 "q-109114" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244238 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244238 1071 "Consider the quadrilateral $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0),(1,0),(1,1)$ and $(0,2),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\,dx+2xy{e}^{xy}\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244238 1072 #uuid "809d1894-c8be-5baf-9170-eb9dccb8466c" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244238 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/b7/b7fad52bdf54c0870ad4c4939a314f5657e390b589faf76e60a8d8ef5d926039.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}$ and $Q(x,y)=2xy{e}^{xy}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=(2y+x{y}^{2}){e}^{xy},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=(2y+2x{y}^{2}){e}^{xy}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\,dx+2xy{e}^{xy}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[(2y+2x{y}^{2}){e}^{xy}-(2y+x{y}^{2}){e}^{xy}]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2y+2x{y}^{2}-2y-x{y}^{2}){e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244239 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243813 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244239 1069 "q-109115" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244239 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244239 1071 "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=(3xy-1)\\,i+(3{x}^{2}+{e}^{y})\\,j$ and $C$ is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $y=2x$ and $y={x}^{2}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244239 1072 #uuid "5f6efefc-16a2-55cb-8b63-0d3253a6815c" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244239 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/5f/5f34ac74599937e0ae181126ff9a3da5b29dfe213614a1f23ca10c7062f22f7d.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(3xy-1)\\,dx+(3{x}^{2}+{e}^{y})\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=3xy-1$ and $Q(x,y)=3{x}^{2}+{e}^{y}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 2x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(3xy-1)\\,dx+(3{x}^{2}+{e}^{y})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(6x-3x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2x}3x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3x[y]_{{x}^{2}}^{2x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3x(2x-{x}^{2})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(6{x}^{2}-3{x}^{3})\\,dx \\\\  & ={[2{x}^{3}-\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2(2)^{3}-\\frac{3(2)^{4}}{4} \\\\  & =16-12 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244240 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243835 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244240 1069 "q-109124" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244240 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244240 1071 "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ that lies between the lines $x=1$ and $x=e.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244240 1072 #uuid "dece3370-94a9-5933-91ea-e0822d5c845c" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244240 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${x}_{1}=1$ to ${x}_{2}=e.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244241 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243805 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244241 1069 "q-109126" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244241 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244241 1071 "![](images/0e/0edb5218c2d1527957053a35e992c7538a8857132a6d48114e16e5f71d3848d6.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $y=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}$ that lies in the first quadrant between the line $x=0$ and its $x$-intercept. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244241 1072 #uuid "815b3056-c4c0-522e-992e-21d3b5a5f29d" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244241 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 4-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}})\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244242 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243797 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244242 1069 "q-109130" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244242 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244242 1071 "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,2),(1,0)$ and $(2,0),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})\\,dx+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244242 1072 #uuid "791b6409-d8b7-533d-ab9f-9fd6eeb81e0a" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244242 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/8e/8e3efc3f9c58fb0f9a84144e97a300b6ab2a4011d47ee0d2bae1edc8a3f721cd.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})\\,dx+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244243 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243713 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244243 1069 "q-109143" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244243 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244243 1071 "Consider the arc of the curve $y=2-\\sqrt{x}$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=4.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244243 1072 #uuid "8775e28e-b78f-5d9e-8282-429406b40780" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244243 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${x}_{1}=0$ to ${x}_{2}=4.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2-\\sqrt{x})=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(2-\\sqrt{x})\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(2-\\sqrt{x})\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(2-\\sqrt{x})\\sqrt{\\frac{4x+1}{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244244 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243685 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244244 1069 "q-109151" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244244 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244244 1071 "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨{e}^{x}-{y}^{2},{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}⟩$ and $C$ is the boundary of the square with vertices at $(-1,-1),$ $(1,-1),$ $(1,1)$ and $(-1,1).$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244244 1072 #uuid "d2d8f7a5-f5ff-573c-8706-8f2ae5d3ecba" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244244 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/57/574172dda3031b9bb44dbcfb46265453ae543a75bccf8db24e93625e7154bba9.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({e}^{y}+{x}^{2})\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{x}-{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{y}+{x}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({e}^{y}+{x}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[2xy+{y}^{2}]}_{-1}^{1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2x(1)+(1)^{2}-2x(-1)-(-1)^{2})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}4x\\,dx \\\\  & =2{[{x}^{2}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2[(1)^{2}-(-1)^{2}] \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244245 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243601 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244245 1069 "q-109152" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244245 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244245 1071 "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,\\,1)$ for the curve $5{x}^{3}-2{y}^{4}=3.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244245 1072 #uuid "f4792eb7-cef8-5807-bfe2-869a28aaef03" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244245 1074 "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{3}-2{y}^{4}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{y}^{4}) & =0 \\\\ 15{x}^{2}-8{y}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ -8{y}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-15{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{15{x}^{2}}{8{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(1,\\,1)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(1,\\,1)} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{15{x}^{2}}{8{y}^{3}})}_{(1,\\,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\cdot {1}^{2}}{8\\cdot {1}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244246 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243684 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244246 1069 "q-109154" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244246 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244246 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x}=\\sqrt{x}\\,{y}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244246 1072 #uuid "48fa1d56-7545-55a4-8175-861ef17502b3" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244246 1074 "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-\\frac{1}{x},\\,q(x)=\\sqrt{x},\\,n=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-2}\\frac{dy}{dx}-\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{-1}=\\sqrt{x}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{x}z=\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z=-\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,Q(x)=-\\sqrt{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z) & =x\\cdot (-\\sqrt{x}) \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z & =-x\\sqrt{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz) & =-{x}^{3/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -{x}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ xz & =-\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-2{x}^{5/2}}{5x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=5{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1}=\\frac{C-2{x}^{5/2}}{5x}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{5x}{C-2{x}^{5/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244247 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243837 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244247 1069 "q-109155" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244247 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244247 1071 "![](images/22/22bb16a874005f09d5cbad055f149f07e2139f559a2a04368c32696567714b26.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}$ that lies in the first quadrant between the line $y=0$ and its $y$-intercept. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244247 1072 #uuid "99e4906b-165d-530b-99fc-6645c7d97282" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244247 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=\\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}y\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}y\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{2-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{2-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243809 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244248 1069 "q-109156" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244248 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244248 1071 "If $x=5{y}^{3}-10{y}^{2},$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244248 1072 #uuid "2e657054-5587-5750-9021-605c700e3136" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244248 1074 "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(5{y}^{3}-10{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3})-10\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =15{y}^{2}-20y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{15{y}^{2}-20y}.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244249 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243513 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244249 1069 "q-109160" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244249 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244249 1071 "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}(2y-x)\\,dx+(5x-y)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $y=x$ and $y={x}^{2}-x.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244249 1072 #uuid "13e80aba-bf74-59e1-8677-06e92124ae97" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244249 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/e7/e788bbcbda4cc715c6989b7de9d08d60e3b0d178eeb9432a342a4c86c4c01b86.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2y-x$ and $Q(x,y)=5x-y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}-x\\le y\\le x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2y-x)\\,dx+(5x-y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(5-2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}-x}^{x}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}[y]_{{x}^{2}-x}^{x}\\,dx \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x-{x}^{2})\\,dx \\\\  & =3{[{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3(4-\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244250 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243507 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244250 1069 "q-109164" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244250 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244250 1071 "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}2xy\\,dx+{y}^{2}\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}$ and $y=\\sqrt{3x}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244250 1072 #uuid "d29c3a3a-1bb9-50bf-8673-d429ca766812" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244250 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/eb/eb0b120cc4d63c8dab18c8acb37f5835e99db746584262f6bb33e8ca99b6a16d.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2xy$ and $Q(x,y)={y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{3x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}2xy\\,dx+{y}^{2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(0-2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}/3}^{\\sqrt{3x}}(-2x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(-2x)[y]_{{x}^{2}/3}^{\\sqrt{3x}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(-2x)(\\sqrt{3x}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3})dx \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{3}(\\sqrt{3}{x}^{3/2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})dx \\\\  & =-2{[\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{3x}}{5}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{12}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-2(\\frac{2(3)^{2}\\sqrt{3(3)}}{5}-\\frac{(3)^{4}}{12}) \\\\  & =-2(\\frac{54}{5}-\\frac{27}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{81}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244251 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243625 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244251 1069 "q-109166" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244251 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244251 1071 "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨2x+{y}^{2},{x}^{2}+2y⟩$ and $C$ is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}$ and the lines $y=0$ and $x=3.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244251 1072 #uuid "7203c090-0409-569d-a6e2-3e62f7801a90" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244251 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/fa/fafd281fc63fb276c9759fd84f722096d494aa3e3fe9441f43aa6ecd1f9dfda9.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(2x+{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+2y)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2x+{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+2y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2x+{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+2y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}/3}(2x-2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[2xy-{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{{x}^{2}/3}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{9})dx \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{6}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{45}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3)^{4}}{6}-\\frac{(3)^{5}}{45} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}-\\frac{27}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244252 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243531 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244252 1069 "q-109170" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244252 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244252 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\nx\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y={x}^{2}{y}^{3/4}.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244252 1072 #uuid "ad4ebdc1-705f-579a-b327-9d9638977692" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244252 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y={x}^{2}{y}^{3/4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{y}{x} & =x{y}^{3/4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{-1},\\,q(x)=x,\\,n=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{3/4},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-3/4}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{-1}{y}^{1/4}=x.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{1/4}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{-3/4}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-3/4}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n4\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{-1}z=x\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-1}z=\\frac{x}{4}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is now in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{1}{4x},\\,Q(x)=\\frac{x}{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/4x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{(1/4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{1/4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={x}^{1/4}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{1/4}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-1}z) & ={x}^{1/4}\\cdot (\\frac{x}{4}) \\\\ {x}^{1/4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/4}z & =\\frac{{x}^{5/4}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{x}^{1/4}) & =\\frac{{x}^{5/4}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{x}^{1/4})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{{x}^{5/4}}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{x}^{1/4} & =\\frac{1}{9}{x}^{9/4}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{{x}^{9/4}+C}{9{x}^{1/4}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=9{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{1/4}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n{y}^{1/4}=\\frac{{x}^{9/4}+C}{9{x}^{1/4}}\\,⇒\\,y={(\\frac{{x}^{9/4}+C}{9{x}^{1/4}})}^{4}\\,.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244253 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243631 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244253 1069 "q-109172" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244253 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244253 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}+2xy}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244253 1072 #uuid "6a2e7c3e-5114-54f0-aa4a-d1c840050f5f" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244253 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}+2xy}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2{x}^{-1}y & ={x}^{-2}{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-2{x}^{-1},\\,q(x)={x}^{-2},\\,n=2.\n$$\n\nNow, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-2}\\frac{dy}{dx}-2{x}^{-1}{y}^{-1}={x}^{-2}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2{x}^{-1}z={x}^{-2}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2{x}^{-1}z=-{x}^{-2}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is now in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{2}{x},\\,Q(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (2/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={x}^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2{x}^{-1}z) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (-{x}^{-2}) \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2xz & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}z) & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}z)\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {x}^{2}z & =-x+C \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-x}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n{y}^{-1}=\\frac{C-x}{{x}^{2}}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{C-x}\\,.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244254 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243788 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244254 1069 "q-109177" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244254 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244254 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}-xy.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244254 1072 #uuid "7c7c063b-3c69-5ffc-9eea-18aa3a923ee9" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244254 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={y}^{-1/2}-xy \\\\ {x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy & ={y}^{-1/2} \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{-1}y & ={x}^{-2}{y}^{-1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{-1},\\,q(x)={x}^{-2},\\,n=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the differential equation by ${y}^{1/2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{1/2}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{-1}{y}^{3/2}={x}^{-2}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{3/2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{1/2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{-1}z={x}^{-2}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1}z=\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-2}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is now in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{3}{2x},\\,Q(x)=\\frac{3}{2{x}^{2}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (3/2x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{(3/2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={x}^{3/2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3/2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1}z) & ={x}^{3/2}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-2}) \\\\ {x}^{3/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2}z & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/2}z) & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/2}z)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {x}^{3/2}z & =3{x}^{1/2}+C \\\\ z & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{x}+C}{{x}^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{3/2}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n{y}^{3/2}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{x}+C}{{x}^{3/2}}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{{(3\\sqrt{x}+C)}^{2/3}}{x}\\,.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244255 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243525 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244255 1069 "q-109188" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244255 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244255 1071 "![](images/9b/9b27f44aa7ef6bef95a88ccfcb6495d17e9457a7452bf602f87dcd5b95ec71dc.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ that lies in the first quadrant between its $y$-intercept and the line $y=e.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244255 1072 #uuid "e38dda82-9f39-5297-8b68-eff9a87b31e9" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244255 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y} & ={e}^{0} \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=1$ to $y=e.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\frac{1}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}y\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244256 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243676 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244256 1069 "q-1092" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244256 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244256 1071 "Calculate the minor of the entry ${b}_{22}$ in the matrix\n$B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 13 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244256 1072 #uuid "926e7b3c-e48b-52a2-9640-544759dee372" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244256 1074 "The minor of ${b}_{22}=7$ (second row, second column) is the determinant of the remaining entries when the column and row containing $7$ are removed: \n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}3 & ∗ & -6 \\\\ ∗ & 7 & ∗ \\\\ 5 & ∗ & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the minor of ${b}_{22}=7$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}] & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 4-(-6)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =12-(-30) \\\\  & =42.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244257 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243453 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244257 1069 "q-109236" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244257 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244257 1071 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A-I$ and $A+4I$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x,$ $y$ and $z$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $w$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$\n- $u$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$\n- $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244257 1072 #uuid "2cf2c42d-88e3-50f2-bb3a-16ab087d4389" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244257 1074 "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since\n\n$$\nw\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-I$ maps $w$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $w$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1,$ not $2.$\n\n- Statement II is  true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\nu\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-I=A-1I$ maps $u$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $u$ is a generalzed eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding  to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\ny\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+4I$ maps $y$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ \ncorresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244258 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243680 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244258 1069 "q-109237" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244258 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244258 1071 "![](images/2b/2baa8f39370b831bd8306aa3eb60e5d16d54ce50474f873f0fa329c069d61689.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ that lies between $y=1$ and $y=e,$ as shown above. What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244258 1072 #uuid "3955735d-411f-53ca-9d80-4ffea69537dd" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244258 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $y=1$ to $y=e.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\frac{1}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{{y}^{2}+1}{{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}}{y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244259 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243572 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244259 1069 "q-109244" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244259 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244259 1071 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A-6I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-6I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-6I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A-6I$ and $A+7I$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x,$ $y$ and $z$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $w$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =6$ of $A$\n- $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7$ of $A$\n- $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7$ of $A$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244259 1072 #uuid "3a61bdaa-5171-5017-8ae8-0bac73d29cdb" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244259 1074 "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since\n\n$$\nw\\overset{A-6I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-6I$ maps $w$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $w$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1,$ not $3.$\n\n- Statement II is  true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\nx\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+7I$ maps $x$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $x$ is a generalzed eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding  to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\ny\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+7I$ maps $y$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ \ncorresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7$ of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244260 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243863 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244260 1069 "q-109249" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244260 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244260 1071 "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $x=y$ and $x=4y-{y}^{2}.$ Express ${∮}_{C}({e}^{y}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dx+(2x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dy$ as a repeated integral." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244260 1072 #uuid "806c47fd-68e8-5b88-97f5-76f2521ac858" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244260 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/23/239867aec8cff7840667d2f4eafa9bc97d4fc5af09b684d863fef06fd8fd28ad.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{y}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=2x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{y}+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2{e}^{y}+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):y\\le x\\le 4y-{y}^{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{y}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dx+(2x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2{e}^{y}+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{e}^{y}-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{y}^{4y-{y}^{2}}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244261 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243687 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244261 1069 "q-109258" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244261 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244261 1071 "![](images/3d/3d01ddb2278875780397e90a9d6f6ad6b2fc44890c59faab7a69b8ebcb5d210b.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=4-(y-4)^{2}$ that lies in the first quadrant between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244261 1072 #uuid "eee8c8a0-68a0-5a7d-816a-fe17e450ddb5" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244261 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-(y-4)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =4 \\\\ y-4 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ y & =2,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=2$ to $y=6.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(4-(y-4)^{2})=8-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{6}(4-(y-4)^{2})\\sqrt{1+{(8-2y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{6}(-{y}^{2}+8y-12)\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}-32y+65}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244262 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243694 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244262 1069 "q-109261" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244262 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244262 1071 "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ that lies between the lines $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=\\sqrt{3}.$ What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244262 1072 #uuid "e36b6b25-8f88-5e08-b750-faf815fb6a2e" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244262 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\sqrt{3}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\sqrt{3}}x\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244263 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243494 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244263 1069 "q-109263" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244263 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244263 1071 "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=x+2.$ Express ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$  where $F(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+x)-{y}^{2},{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}⟩,$ as a repeated integral." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244263 1072 #uuid "a5b7043e-fa21-5314-98d6-598e5c27911d" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244263 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/e3/e32b8e7d6ce80d7cedad928f9a2f7acc3c3d7f06add7909e856db27d154068d6.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+x)-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+x)-{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x+2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244264 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243503 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244264 1069 "q-109266" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244264 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244264 1071 "Find $\\,|\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & x & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & y & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}|$ given that $\\,|\\begin{aligned}x & -1 \\\\ 5 & y\\end{aligned}|=-3.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244264 1072 #uuid "1e0f9240-ada8-57e3-92f8-c3e520c31920" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244264 1074 "Notice that we have a block upper triangular matrix. Therefore, its determinant is equal to the product of the diagonal blocks' determinants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & x & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & y & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & x & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & y & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =6\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}x & -1 \\\\ 5 & y\\end{aligned}|\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =6\\cdot (-3)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-90.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244265 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243543 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244265 1069 "q-109267" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244265 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244265 1071 "Find $\\,|\\begin{aligned}v & w & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 5\\end{aligned}|$ given that $\\,|\\begin{aligned}v & w \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}|=3.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244265 1072 #uuid "548f0040-ec44-5816-9b73-e986b0aab71c" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244265 1074 "Notice that we have a block diagonal matrix. Therefore, its determinant is equal to the product of the diagonal blocks' determinants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}v & w & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 5\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}v & w & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}v & w \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}|\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244266 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243557 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244266 1069 "q-109270" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244266 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244266 1071 "Find $\\,|\\begin{aligned}x & 1 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 2 & y & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & z & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|$ given that $\\,|\\begin{aligned}x & 1 & 10 \\\\ 2 & y & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & z\\end{aligned}|=8.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244266 1072 #uuid "6013dd11-16ea-5817-8be8-82c1e6b03532" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244266 1074 "Notice that we have a block diagonal matrix. Therefore, its determinant is equal to the product of the diagonal blocks' determinants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}x & 1 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 2 & y & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & z & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}x & 1 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 2 & y & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & z & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}x & 1 & 10 \\\\ 2 & y & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & z\\end{aligned}|\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =8\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244267 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243521 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244267 1069 "q-109271" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244267 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244267 1071 "Find $\\,|\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & m & b & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & n & 0 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|$ given that $\\,|\\begin{aligned}m & b \\\\ 0 & n\\end{aligned}|=-6.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244267 1072 #uuid "3f16b598-5185-592e-ad1f-83437e5d73ec" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244267 1074 "Notice that we have a block lower triangular matrix. Therefore, its determinant is equal to the product of the diagonal blocks' determinants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & m & b & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & n & 0 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & m & b & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & n & 0 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}m & b \\\\ 0 & n\\end{aligned}|\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-6)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-72.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244268 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243660 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244268 1069 "q-109273" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244268 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244268 1071 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A+8I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A+8I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+8I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A+8I$ and $A$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x$ and $y$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is a generalized  eigenvector of rank $1$  corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-8$ of $A$\n- $u$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-8$ of $A$\n- $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of $A$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244268 1072 #uuid "1122434b-dbdd-5a52-98a7-34990f65e51f" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244268 1074 "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I  is false. Since\n\n$$\nv\\overset{A+8I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+8I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+8I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ not $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\nu\\overset{A+8I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A+8I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+8I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+8I$ maps $u$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $u$ is an generalized eigenvector of rank $3$  corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-8$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since\n\n$$\ny\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A$ maps $y$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ (a.k.a. an eigenvector), not $2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244269 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243480 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244269 1069 "q-109274" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244269 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244269 1071 "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)=(t-3){e}^{t},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244269 1072 #uuid "6ea54c4a-8ef8-5e3e-86fc-467854dde178" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244269 1074 "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t-3){e}^{t}) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}+(t-3){e}^{t} \\\\  & =(t-2){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we set $v=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t-2){e}^{t} & =0 \\\\ t-2 & =0 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244270 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243657 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244270 1069 "q-109275" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244270 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244270 1071 "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}-6t+10),\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244270 1072 #uuid "86745f4a-9b3a-5cb4-90c7-0e4ceefd13ac" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244270 1074 "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}-6t+10)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2t-6}{{t}^{2}-6t+10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(t-3)}{{t}^{2}-6t+10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we set $v=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(t-3)}{{t}^{2}-6t+10} & =0 \\\\ 2(t-3) & =0 \\\\ t & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=3\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244271 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243597 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244271 1069 "q-109276" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244271 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244271 1071 "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)={t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244271 1072 #uuid "bffd97ab-bc2b-576c-a77d-6d2d7cde8ae6" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244271 1074 "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t) \\\\  & =2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+{t}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{t} \\\\  & =2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+t \\\\  & =t(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we set $v=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+1) & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+1 & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡t & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t & ={e}^{-1/2} \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244272 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243555 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244272 1069 "q-109279" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244272 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244272 1071 "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)={ln}^{2}⁡t+2,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244272 1072 #uuid "44bcce29-44a3-5949-85ff-55ab7d8ed22b" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244272 1074 "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({ln}^{2}⁡t+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we set $v=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡t & =0 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=1\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244273 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243501 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244273 1069 "q-109289" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244273 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244273 1071 "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=\\sqrt{t}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,$ where $t>0$ is the time measured in seconds. Determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244273 1072 #uuid "8de2ca9e-4b83-5dd2-84c1-5e6128a2ad43" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244273 1074 "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x,$ then its velocity is negative. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{2\\sqrt{t}}+\\frac{\\sqrt{t}}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{2\\sqrt{t}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$, we solve the inequality $v(t)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2}{2\\sqrt{t}} & <0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2 & <0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡t & <-2 \\\\ t & <{e}^{-2} \\\\ t & <\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of decreasing $x$ is $t\\in (0,\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}).$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244274 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243700 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244274 1069 "q-109290" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244274 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244274 1071 "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=t{e}^{-t},$ where $t>0$ is the time measured in seconds. Determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244274 1072 #uuid "9b357d66-de0f-577d-8e07-59b5c85bfb2e" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244274 1074 "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x,$ then its velocity is negative. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}-t{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =(1-t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$, we solve the inequality $v(t)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ (1-t){e}^{-t} & <0 \\\\ 1-t & <0 \\\\ t & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of decreasing $x$ is $t\\in (1,\\infty ).$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244275 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243534 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244275 1069 "q-109300" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244275 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244275 1071 "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=(t-3){e}^{t},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244275 1072 #uuid "8572daa3-cc03-5208-a223-17b8d59a3e82" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244275 1074 "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t-3){e}^{t}) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}+(t-3){e}^{t} \\\\  & =(t-2){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t-2){e}^{t}) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}+(t-2){e}^{t} \\\\  & =(t-1){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t-1){e}^{t} & =0 \\\\ t-1 & =0 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=1.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\nv(1)=(1-2){e}^{1}=-e.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244276 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243463 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244276 1069 "q-109303" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244276 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244276 1071 "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=\\frac{{t}^{3}}{2}-6{t}^{2}+30t+4,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244276 1072 #uuid "e46d1d2b-7388-5f25-9acc-1ef9f7d0ffa9" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244276 1074 "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{2}-6{t}^{2}+30t+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2}-12t+30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2}-12t+30) \\\\  & =3t-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3t-12 & =0 \\\\ 3t & =12 \\\\ t & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the acceleration is zero when $t=4.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(4) & =\\frac{3(4)^{2}}{2}-12(4)+30 \\\\  & =24-48+30 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244277 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243705 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244277 1069 "q-109315" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244277 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244277 1071 "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244277 1072 #uuid "aa8294c2-0b9e-5133-97e1-eef81a69651e" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244277 1074 "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-2\\cdot (-2) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -5)(\\lambda -5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=5.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(5)=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244278 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243707 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244278 1069 "q-109316" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244278 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244278 1071 "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244278 1072 #uuid "52870c2b-5862-5c0b-884f-72d410941954" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244278 1074 "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda )-(2)(2) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable, and a Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{1}(-3)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(2)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244279 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243544 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244279 1069 "q-109319" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244279 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244279 1071 "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}$ that lies between the lines $y=0$ and $y=2.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244279 1072 #uuid "c817dfb7-cfc8-5d1c-9306-1a0f55b185a8" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244279 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${y}_{1}=0$ to ${y}_{2}=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9-{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\frac{3y}{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =3\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2y}{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the substitution \n\n$$\nu=9-{y}^{2}\\,⇒\\,\\text{d}u=-2y\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =3\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2y}{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-3\\pi {\\int }_{9}^{5}{u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\pi {\\int }_{5}^{9}{u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\pi {[2\\sqrt{u}\\,]}_{5}^{9} \\\\  & =6\\pi (3-\\sqrt{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244280 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243450 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244280 1069 "q-109360" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244280 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244280 1071 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A+9I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A+9I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+9I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A+9I$ and $A-I$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x,$ $y$ and $z$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-9$ of $A$\n- $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$\n- $z$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244280 1072 #uuid "65e6b2ee-a472-5db0-bf96-384d33845a1c" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244280 1074 "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false.  Since \n\n$$\nv\\overset{A+9I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+9I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\n and $v\\ne 0,$ \ni.e. $A+9I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ not $1.$\n\n- Statement II is  false. Since \n\n$$\nx\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\n \ni.e. $A-I$ maps $x$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3,$ not $2.$\n\n- Statement III is true.  Indeed, since \n\n$$\nz\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-1I$ maps $z$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $w$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244281 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243740 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244281 1069 "q-109363" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244281 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244281 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & ∗ \\\\ b & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{a}{b}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244281 1072 #uuid "b3283580-5a59-5591-b6e2-a7b4ead9a8ca" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244281 1074 "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ corresponds to an eigenvector of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-(-3)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}]-(-3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244282 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243475 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244282 1069 "q-109364" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244282 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244282 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & ∗ \\\\ b & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{b}{a}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244282 1072 #uuid "d21971e7-3553-5c30-b33e-2ba71b3dd2f7" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244282 1074 "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ corresponds to an eigenvector of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-(2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244283 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243692 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244283 1069 "q-109365" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244283 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244283 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & ∗ \\\\ b & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{b}{a}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244283 1072 #uuid "bd78e648-49a9-5a9b-9b15-2651f4d8a066" 13194139533393 17592186045585 true] [17592186244283 1074 "From the Jordan matrix $J,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ corresponds to an eigenvector of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1.$ \n\nNow, computing 